    "timingType": "Double-roller chain. By this late production era of the M102 engine, Mercedes-Benz had upgraded the timing system to a robust duplex chain. While highly durable, the plastic guide rails become extremely brittle over thirty years and must be replaced to prevent them from shattering into the oil pan.",
    "injectionType": "Bosch KE-Jetronic (CIS-E). A continuous mechanical fuel injection system with electronic mixture control. This specific variant includes a factory catalytic converter and lambda (O2) sensor integration to meet early Swiss and German emissions standards, which slightly restricts airflow and output.",
    "engineLifespan": "300 000 - 500 000+ km. The cast iron block and forged internals of the M102 are legendary for their unyielding durability. The true lifespan is dictated entirely by the owner's ability to maintain the finicky KE-Jetronic fuel system and the lambda control loop.",
    "turboType": "Naturally Aspirated. It completely lacks forced induction, offering an incredibly reliable, old-school power delivery. The absence of a turbocharger vastly simplifies the engine bay and drastically improves overall long-term mechanical reliability under harsh off-road abuse.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch KE-Jetronic Control Unit. A highly rudimentary analog computer that manages the cold start enrichment, the Electro-Hydraulic Actuator valve, and closed-loop lambda control. Because the system is predominantly mechanical, traditional software tuning is physically impossible.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 1 (Catalyst Equipped)",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "250+ hp. The incredibly robust cast iron block and heavy-duty forged internals are massively over-engineered for the paltry 122 horsepower stock output. Achieving high horsepower requires abandoning the KE-Jetronic system entirely in favor of a modern standalone EFI conversion.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "16.5 - 19.0 L/100km",
      "highway": "12.5 - 14.5 L/100km",
      "mixed": "15.0 - 17.0 L/100km",
      "sporty": "21.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "6.0L 10W-40 Fully Synthetic. Absolute adherence to strict 10,000 km oil change intervals is mandatory to protect the camshaft lobes and the hydraulic lifters from severe friction wear.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "KE-Jetronic EHA Valve Leak: The Electro-Hydraulic Actuator valve attached to the fuel distributor frequently develops internal leaks over decades of use. This causes raw fuel to drip onto the hot engine block and leads to difficult warm starts.",
      "Overvoltage Protection Relay Failure: The OVP relay is responsible for protecting the delicate KE-Jetronic ECU from voltage spikes. When the internal solders crack, the engine loses idle control, ABS lights illuminate, and the car runs exceptionally poorly.",
      "Valve Stem Seal Degradation: The rubber valve stem seals harden and crack due to age and thermal cycling. This allows engine oil to seep down the valve guides into the combustion chamber overnight, causing a distinct puff of blue smoke upon cold startup.",
      "Distributor Cap and Rotor Moisture: The traditional distributor cap and ignition rotor are highly susceptible to moisture ingress and carbon tracking. This leads to weak spark delivery, severe misfires under load, and poor fuel economy in wet weather conditions.",
      "Vacuum System Leaks: The Bosch KE-Jetronic system relies heavily on absolute vacuum integrity. The rubber vacuum hoses and the large rubber boot under the air flow meter become brittle and crack, creating massive unmetered air leaks.",
      "Lambda Sensor Aging: The O2 sensor degrades over time, causing the KE-Jetronic system to run rich. This drastically lowers fuel economy and can quickly overheat and destroy the expensive factory catalytic converter."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Catastrophic Engine Fire via Fuel Lines: The aging rubber fuel hoses connecting the fuel distributor to the cold start valve become severely degraded and brittle. Under high pressure, these hoses can rupture and spray raw gasoline directly onto the exhaust manifold.",
      "Fuel Distributor Plunger Seizure: If the vehicle sits for extended periods or is run on poor-quality fuel, the central plunger inside the fuel distributor can rust and seize completely, disabling the mechanical fuel injection system.",
      "Catalytic Converter Meltdown: If the ignition system misfires or the Lambda sensor fails causing a rich mixture, unburnt fuel will dump into the exhaust. This ignites inside the catalytic converter, melting the precious metal honeycomb and blocking the exhaust entirely.",
      "Head Gasket Oil Leaks: The M102 engine is historically prone to developing severe oil leaks at the right rear corner of the cylinder head gasket. Pressurized engine oil seeps past the degraded gasket material, fouling the transmission bellhousing.",
      "Camshaft Lobe Wear: On engines subjected to poor maintenance or extended oil change intervals, the camshaft lobes and rocker arms suffer from severe friction wear. A loud, rhythmic tapping noise indicates irreversible valvetrain damage.",
      "Differential Locker Actuator Seizing: The W463 G-Class utilizes hydraulic actuators to engage the legendary differential locks. If the system is not exercised regularly, the actuators seize internally and the metal lines rust, disabling off-road capabilities."
    ],
