    "timingType": "Single-roller chain (Simplex). Unlike the newer M104 engines, the M103 utilizes a single-row timing chain. While rarely snapping catastrophically, it is highly prone to physical stretching over 200,000 kilometers. This alters the engine's mechanical timing, causing sluggish performance and a distinct metallic rattle. Replacement and monitoring are necessary for long-term health.",
    "injectionType": "Bosch KE-Jetronic (CIS-E). A continuous mechanical fuel injection system paired with electronic mixture control. Delivering fuel to six cylinders, the massive fuel distributor is highly complex and incredibly sensitive to vacuum leaks, degraded rubber boots, and dirty fuel.",
    "engineLifespan": "300 000 - 500 000+ km. The cast iron inline-six block is phenomenally robust and perfectly balanced. The true lifespan is entirely dictated by managing the notorious head gasket leaks (specifically at the front timing cover) and keeping the intricate KE-Jetronic system perfectly sealed.",
    "turboType": "Naturally Aspirated. It provides a beautifully smooth, linear torque curve perfectly suited for off-roading, completely lacking the complexity and heat of modern turbochargers.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch KE-Jetronic Control Unit. Manages the closed-loop Lambda system and idle control. Because fuel delivery is physically dictated by a mechanical airflow meter plate, traditional software ECU tuning is entirely impossible.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 1",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "350+ hp. The block and rotating assembly are massively strong. Historically, period-correct twin-turbo kits (like those from Mosselman) pushed these engines reliably to 300+ horsepower, though modern standalone EFI conversions make this much safer and more efficient.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "18.0 - 22.0 L/100km",
      "highway": "14.0 - 16.5 L/100km",
      "mixed": "16.5 - 18.5 L/100km",
      "sporty": "24.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "6.0L 10W-40 Fully Synthetic. Absolute adherence to strict 10,000 km oil change intervals is mandatory to protect the single-row timing chain and the single overhead camshaft from excessive friction wear.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Front Timing Cover Oil Leak: The absolute most famous flaw of the M103. The 'U-shaped' rubber seal between the timing cover and the cylinder head hardens and fails, dumping engine oil down the front of the engine block. Fixing it properly often requires lifting the cylinder head slightly or complete removal.",
      "Head Gasket Failure: The long aluminum cylinder head mounted to the iron block is prone to thermal expansion differences. Over decades, the head gasket frequently fails at the rear right corner (leaking oil externally) or between cylinders (mixing oil and coolant).",
      "Valve Stem Seal Degradation: A hallmark of the M103. The rubber valve stem seals harden and crack, allowing oil to run down the valves overnight. Upon cold startup, the engine will emit a massive, embarrassing cloud of blue smoke. Replacing the seals cures the massive oil consumption.",
      "KE-Jetronic Vacuum Leaks: The entire fuel injection system relies on a massive rubber 'bathtub' boot situated under the fuel distributor. When this boot cracks with age, it creates a colossal vacuum leak, making the engine stall, surge, and run incredibly lean.",
      "Distributor Cap Moisture: The traditional mechanical distributor is mounted at the very front of the engine. It is highly susceptible to catching moisture from the radiator fan or deep puddles, leading to severe misfires, bucking, and a complete loss of power.",
      "Overvoltage Protection (OVP) Relay: The OVP relay protects the ECU from voltage spikes. When its internal solders crack, the idle control valve stops working, ABS lights turn on, and the engine refuses to idle properly."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Catastrophic Engine Fire via Fuel Lines: The aging rubber fuel hoses connecting the massive KE-Jetronic fuel distributor to the injectors and cold start valve become brittle. Under 5.5 bar of pressure, a ruptured line will spray raw gasoline directly onto the hot exhaust manifold, causing a total vehicle fire.",
      "Coolant and Oil Mixing (Milkshake): If the head gasket fails between an oil and coolant passage, the two fluids will mix, creating a thick, brown, milkshake-like sludge in the radiator and oil pan. This instantly destroys the engine's lubricating ability, wiping out the main bearings.",
      "Simplex Timing Chain Stretch: Because the M103 uses a single-row timing chain, it stretches significantly more than the later M104 duplex chains. If left unchecked, the loose chain will chew through the plastic guide rails, throwing plastic shards into the oil pump pickup tube and starving the engine of oil.",
      "Fuel Distributor Plunger Seizure: If the vehicle sits unused for years, the central control plunger inside the fuel distributor will rust and seize. This completely disables fuel delivery, and rebuilding the unit requires microscopic precision and a massive budget.",
      "Differential Locker Actuator Seizing: The W463 G-Class utilizes hydraulic actuators to engage the front, center, and rear differential locks. If the system is not exercised regularly, the actuators seize internally and the metal lines rust, completely disabling the vehicle's off-road capabilities."
    ],
