    "timingType": "Single-roller chain (Simplex). Unlike the newer M104 engines, the M103 utilizes a single-row timing chain. While rarely snapping catastrophically, it is highly prone to physical stretching over 200,000 kilometers. This alters the engine's mechanical timing, causing sluggish performance and a distinct metallic rattle. Replacement and monitoring are necessary for long-term health.",
    "injectionType": "Bosch KE-Jetronic (CIS-E). A continuous mechanical fuel injection system paired with electronic mixture control. This specific variant was calibrated for strict early US and Japanese emissions standards, utilizing a more restrictive catalytic converter and lambda control loop that slightly detuned the engine's output.",
    "engineLifespan": "300 000 - 500 000+ miles. The cast iron inline-six block is phenomenally robust and perfectly balanced. The true lifespan is entirely dictated by managing the notorious head gasket leaks (specifically at the front timing cover) and keeping the intricate KE-Jetronic system perfectly sealed from vacuum leaks.",
    "turboType": "Naturally Aspirated. It provides a beautifully smooth, linear torque curve perfectly suited for off-roading, completely lacking the complexity and heat of modern turbochargers.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch KE-Jetronic Control Unit. Manages the closed-loop Lambda system and idle control. Because fuel delivery is physically dictated by a mechanical airflow meter plate, traditional software ECU tuning is entirely impossible.",
