    "oilSpecification": "8.0L 5W-40 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.5). Absolute adherence to strict 5,000 to 7,500-mile oil change intervals is highly recommended to protect the hydraulic lifters and ensure the variable intake manifold flaps do not gum up with oil vapor.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Harmonic Balancer (Crank Pulley) Failure: The absolute most critical flaw of the M113 architecture. The rubber vibration dampener located inside the main crankshaft pulley deteriorates over time. The outer ring separates and violently saws directly into the aluminum timing cover, causing massive oil leaks.",
      "Valve Cover Gasket Leaks: Due to the extreme heat generated by the massive V8 in the tightly packed G-Class engine bay, the rubber valve cover gaskets harden and leak. Oil seeps down onto the hot exhaust manifolds, causing a strong burning smell.",
      "EVAP System Purge Valve Failure: Unique to the strict US emissions software, the EVAP purge valve frequently fails or sticks open. This causes persistent 'Check Engine' lights and slightly rough idling, requiring an inexpensive but annoying replacement.",
      "Twin-Spark Ignition Misfires: The M113 utilizes an incredibly complex twin-spark ignition system (two spark plugs per cylinder, 16 total). The ignition wires frequently degrade, causing misfires that destroy the catalytic converters. Replacing 16 spark plugs is highly labor-intensive.",
      "Crankshaft Position Sensor (CPS) Failure: The CPS, located at the very rear of the engine block near the transmission bellhousing, frequently fails due to intense thermal cycling. A failing CPS will cause sudden stalling when the engine is hot and completely prevent restarting until it cools down.",
      "Mass Airflow Sensor (MAF) Degradation: The Bosch MAF sensor degrades slowly over time, causing hesitation, poor fuel economy, and random 'Check Engine' lights long before it completely fails. Requires an OEM Bosch replacement."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Catastrophic Timing Cover Destruction: If the harmonic balancer fails and the outer ring begins to wobble, it acts like a buzzsaw against the soft aluminum timing cover. If ignored, it will cut completely through the cover, destroying the engine.",
      "Catalytic Converter Meltdown: The M113 is highly sensitive to misfires caused by aging ignition coils or spark plug wires. Unburnt fuel dumps into the exhaust and instantly melts the expensive internal honeycomb of the US-spec catalytic converters.",
      "Rear Main Seal Oil Leaks: If the PCV (Positive Crankcase Ventilation) system clogs with sludge, excessive internal crankcase pressure builds up. This pressure forcefully blows out the rear main seal, dumping oil between the engine and transmission.",
      "Differential Locker Actuator Seizing: The W463 G-Class utilizes hydraulic actuators to engage the front, center, and rear differential locks. If the system is not exercised regularly, the actuators seize internally and the metal lines rust, disabling off-road capabilities.",
      "Transfer Case Actuator Motor Failure: On later M113 models, the electronic shift motor responsible for engaging low range can burn out or its internal plastic gears can strip, preventing the vehicle from shifting into or out of low range."
    ],
