    "timingType": "Double-roller chain. By 2008, the catastrophic idler gear sprocket failure that notoriously destroyed early M273 engines (2006-2007) had been completely and permanently resolved by the factory. The timing chain system on these later 5.5L models is incredibly robust and easily capable of lasting the lifetime of the engine, provided strict oil change intervals are maintained.",
    "injectionType": "Multi-Port Fuel Injection (MPI). Unlike the newer, highly complex direct-injected (CGI) engines, this naturally aspirated block injects fuel directly into the intake manifold before the intake valves. This highly reliable setup completely eliminates the modern nightmare of thick carbon sludge buildup on the intake valves, vastly reducing long-term maintenance costs.",
    "engineLifespan": "300 000 - 500 000+ km. The naturally aspirated, port-injected M273 5.5L V8 is widely regarded as one of the most incredibly durable and reliable modern V8 engines Mercedes-Benz has ever produced. Its true lifespan in the G-Class chassis depends entirely on fixing minor oil leaks and proactively upgrading the fragile plastic intake manifold flaps.",
    "turboType": "Naturally Aspirated. It entirely lacks the complex forced induction systems, highly pressurized intercoolers, and fragile high-pressure fuel pumps of modern biturbo V8s. This offers an incredibly reliable, highly linear power delivery and an immediately crisp, roaring, old-school V8 throttle response.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch ME9.7. A highly reliable and exceptionally well-understood engine management unit. Because the engine is naturally aspirated, aftermarket ECU tuning provides very minimal peak horsepower gains, mostly limited to sharpening the electronic throttle response and optimizing ignition timing.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5 / US EPA Tier 2",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "450+ hp. The internal forged connecting rods and massive cast aluminum block are heavily over-engineered for the stock 388 horsepower. However, significantly increasing power requires custom forced induction, which is absolutely not economically viable compared to simply purchasing a supercharged G 55 AMG.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "18.0 - 22.0 L/100km",
      "highway": "13.0 - 15.0 L/100km",
      "mixed": "15.0 - 17.5 L/100km",
      "sporty": "24.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "8.5L 5W-40 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.5). Regular 10,000 km (or 6,000 mile) oil change intervals are highly recommended to protect the timing chain guides and the complex variable valve timing mechanisms.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Intake Manifold Tumble Flap Failure: The variable intake manifold utilizes incredibly fragile plastic arms to actuate the internal tumble flaps. Due to constant heat cycling and oil vapor exposure from the massive 5.5L V8, these plastic arms become brittle and snap entirely. This restricts engine breathing, causes terrible fuel economy, and triggers a persistent check engine light.",
      "Camshaft Adjuster Magnet Leaks: The four circular camshaft adjuster magnets located at the front of the cylinder heads are totally notorious for developing internal seal leaks. Engine oil slowly pushes through the seal and drips directly into the electrical connector, traveling entirely through the wiring harness.",
      "Oil Centrifuge / Separator Leaks: The PCV system utilizes a plastic oil centrifuge cover located at the rear of the right cylinder head. The factory sealant degrades over time, causing engine oil to drip down the back of the engine block, burning violently on the hot exhaust piping.",
      "Thermostat Sticking Open: The engine coolant thermostat is a highly common wear item that frequently fails mechanically in the open position. This prevents the massive V8 engine from reaching its optimal operating temperature, forcing the ECU to run a rich fuel mixture.",
      "Secondary Air Injection Pump Failure: The electric secondary air pump, responsible for pumping air into the exhaust on cold starts to heat the catalytic converters, frequently suffers from internal bearing failure. It will emit a loud, whining vacuum-cleaner noise before seizing entirely.",
      "Engine Mount Collapse: The liquid-filled hydraulic engine mounts are subjected to the immense, heavy weight and massive low-end torque of the 5.5L V8 block. Over 100,000 kilometers, the rubber tears and the internal hydraulic fluid leaks out, causing a harsh, brutal vibration to resonate through the cabin."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "ECU Destruction via Oil Wicking: If the leaking camshaft adjuster magnets are completely ignored, capillary action will flawlessly draw the engine oil through the wiring harness directly into the main Bosch ECU. Once the oil floods the delicate circuitry of the control unit, it shorts out, requiring a massively expensive ECU replacement.",
      "Catastrophic Intake Manifold Ingestion: In severe cases of intake manifold degradation, the internal plastic tumble flaps can break apart entirely inside the plenum. If a piece of the broken plastic flap is sucked through the intake port and into the combustion chamber, it will cause catastrophic valve and piston damage.",
      "Conductor Plate Failure (7G-Tronic): The 722.9 automatic transmission houses its Transmission Control Module (TCM/Conductor Plate) inside the extremely hot oil pan. The delicate speed sensors on this plate frequently fail due to intense heat, instantly throwing the transmission into Limp Mode.",
      "Rear Main Seal Oil Leaks: If the PCV oil centrifuge system becomes severely clogged with sludge, it creates excessive internal crankcase pressure. This pressure will forcefully blow out the rear main seal, dumping the engine's oil supply between the engine block and the transmission bellhousing.",
      "Catalytic Converter Meltdown: If the thermostat fails open and the engine constantly runs a rich fuel mixture, the unburnt fuel will pass directly into the exhaust system. Over time, this excess fuel ignites inside the catalytic converters, melting the precious metal honeycomb structure.",
      "Transfer Case Actuator Motor Failure: The electronic shift motor responsible for engaging low range can burn out or its internal plastic gears can strip entirely, permanently preventing the vehicle from shifting into or out of low range during off-road excursions."
    ],
