    "timingType": "Double-roller chain. The M157 utilizes an incredibly long and complex timing chain system. Early to mid-production engines are notorious for chain stretch and camshaft adjuster rattle upon cold startup due to hydraulic oil draining out overnight. Retrofitting factory oil non-return check valves and updated tensioners is absolutely mandatory to prevent engine timing failure.",
    "injectionType": "Direct Injection (CGI) operating at up to 200 bar via highly precise Piezo injectors. This extreme pressure ensures phenomenal fuel atomization, but it guarantees thick, tar-like carbon buildup on the intake valves over time since fuel no longer washes over them.",
    "engineLifespan": "200 000 - 300 000+ km. Mechanically, the engine block handles massive horsepower, but the true lifespan is severely threatened by catastrophic cylinder wall scoring (Silitec degradation) caused by leaking fuel injectors or prolonged oil change intervals. Preventative maintenance is everything.",
    "turboType": "Twin-Turbochargers (BiTurbo). Unlike the newer 'Hot-V' engines, the M157 utilizes a traditional layout with massive Garrett turbochargers mounted on the exterior exhaust manifolds. They provide absolutely brutal mid-range punch and a thunderous AMG V8 roar.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch MED17.7.3. A highly complex and powerful engine management unit that tightly controls dual boost pressures and direct injection. This ECU is famously receptive to software tuning, unlocking massive, supercar-slaying power reserves with a simple flash.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "750+ hp. The internal forged connecting rods, forged crankshaft, and robust aluminum block are heavily over-engineered. The block handles massive boost effortlessly, with tuning limits dictated strictly by the transmission torque converter limits and cooling capacity.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "18.0 - 22.0 L/100km",
      "highway": "13.0 - 15.0 L/100km",
      "mixed": "15.0 - 18.0 L/100km",
      "sporty": "25.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "8.5L 5W-40 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.5). Absolute adherence to strict 8,000 to 10,000 km oil change intervals is absolutely mandatory to protect the highly stressed timing chain, twin-turbos, and prevent catastrophic cylinder scoring.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Cold Start Timing Rattle: The most famous M157 flaw. Oil drains out of the secondary chain tensioners and camshaft adjusters overnight. Upon cold start, the engine emits a loud metallic rattle for 3-5 seconds. Installing factory non-return check valves is required.",
      "Cylinder Wall Scoring: The aluminum cylinders feature a Silitec coating. If a Piezo fuel injector leaks or if oil is not changed frequently, the oil film washes away, causing the piston rings to severely score the cylinder walls. This results in massive oil consumption and requires a totally rebuilt, sleeved engine.",
      "Camshaft Sensor Oil Leaking: The camshaft position sensors and adjuster magnets frequently leak engine oil directly into the electrical harness. This oil travels via capillary action straight into the ECU, frying it completely.",
      "Direct Injection Carbon Buildup: Because fuel is injected directly into the cylinders, it never washes the intake valves. Thick carbon sludge builds up rapidly on the valve stems, causing random misfires and restricting airflow. Walnut blasting is required.",
      "Turbocharger Coolant Line Leaks: The plastic and rubber coolant lines feeding the twin turbochargers bake in the extreme heat of the engine bay. They become brittle and crack, leaking coolant down the side of the engine block.",
      "High-Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP) Failure: The camshaft-driven HPFPs work immensely hard to support 200 bar of pressure. They wear out internally, causing extended cranking times, severe power loss, and potentially leaking fuel into the engine oil."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Catastrophic Engine Block Destruction: Cylinder scoring is the ultimate death sentence for an M157. Once the Silitec coating is scratched, the engine will consume massive amounts of oil and emit a heavy knocking sound. Repairing this requires a $15,000+ engine teardown to sleeve the block with steel or Darton sleeves.",
      "Catastrophic Timing Chain Jump: If the cold-start chain rattle is ignored for too long, the timing chain will physically stretch beyond the tensioner's limits, eventually jumping teeth and causing the pistons to strike the valves.",
      "ECU Destruction via Oil Wicking: Leaking camshaft sensors will perfectly channel engine oil directly into the main Bosch ECU. Once the oil floods the delicate circuitry, the vehicle will stall and require a massively expensive ECU replacement.",
      "Transmission Torque Overload: The massive 760 Nm of factory torque places immense, continuous mechanical strain on the 7G-Tronic transmission clutches. Bypassing the strict fluid service interval practically guarantees premature torque converter and clutch pack failure."
    ],
