    "commonIssues": [
      "Cylinder Wall Scoring: The aluminum cylinders feature a Silitec coating. If a Piezo fuel injector leaks or if oil is not changed frequently, the oil film washes away, causing the piston rings to severely score the cylinder walls. This results in massive oil consumption and requires a totally rebuilt, sleeved engine.",
      "Camshaft Sensor Oil Leaking: The camshaft position sensors and adjuster magnets frequently leak engine oil directly into the electrical harness. This oil travels via capillary action straight into the ECU, frying it completely.",
      "Cold Start Timing Rattle: Even with updated parts, oil can drain out of the secondary chain tensioners overnight on high-mileage engines. Upon cold start, the engine emits a loud metallic rattle for 3-5 seconds.",
      "Direct Injection Carbon Buildup: Because fuel is injected directly into the cylinders, it never washes the intake valves. Thick carbon sludge builds up rapidly on the valve stems, causing random misfires and restricting airflow. Walnut blasting is required.",
      "Turbocharger Coolant Line Leaks: The plastic and rubber coolant lines feeding the twin turbochargers bake in the extreme heat of the engine bay. They become brittle and crack, leaking coolant down the side of the engine block.",
      "High-Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP) Failure: The camshaft-driven HPFPs work immensely hard to support 200 bar of pressure. They wear out internally, causing extended cranking times, severe power loss, and potentially leaking fuel into the engine oil."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Catastrophic Engine Block Destruction: Cylinder scoring is the ultimate death sentence for an M157. Once the Silitec coating is scratched, the engine will consume massive amounts of oil and emit a heavy knocking sound. Repairing this requires a $15,000+ engine teardown to sleeve the block with steel or Darton sleeves.",
      "ECU Destruction via Oil Wicking: Leaking camshaft sensors will perfectly channel engine oil directly into the main Bosch ECU. Once the oil floods the delicate circuitry, the vehicle will stall and require a massively expensive ECU replacement.",
      "Catastrophic Timing Chain Jump: If the cold-start chain rattle is ignored for too long, the timing chain will physically stretch beyond the tensioner's limits, eventually jumping teeth and causing the pistons to strike the valves.",
      "Transmission Torque Overload: The massive 760 Nm of factory torque places immense, continuous mechanical strain on the 7G-Tronic transmission clutches. Bypassing the strict fluid service interval practically guarantees premature torque converter and clutch pack failure."
    ],
