    "timingType": "Double-roller chain (Duplex). The M275 utilizes an incredibly robust and massively thick duplex timing chain setup driven from the front. Because this massive V12 operates at a relatively conservative maximum engine speed from the factory, timing chain stretch or premature structural wear is virtually nonexistent.",
    "injectionType": "Multi-Port Fuel Injection (MPI). Unlike contemporary V8 models, the twin-turbocharged V12 purposefully retains port fuel injection. This traditional design completely eliminates the widespread modern issue of thick carbon sludge accumulation on the intake valves, keeping the intake runners pristine.",
    "engineLifespan": "250 000 - 400 000+ km. Mechanically, the massive cast iron blocks and heavy-duty forged components inside the M275 are virtually immortal masterpiece structures. However, its overall operational lifespan is entirely dictated by managing the high preventative cost of the complex dual ignition coils.",
    "turboType": "Twin-Turbochargers (BiTurbo). Features twin liquid-cooled turbochargers mounted directly to heavy-duty cast exhaust manifolds. They generate an absolute mountain of torque (1000 Nm) almost instantly from idle, but pack immense thermal density into the tight, unyielding W463 engine compartment layout.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch Motronic ME7.1.1. A highly complex dual-processor engine management controller built specifically to coordinate the intricate multi-spark coil packs, electronic wastegates, and aggressive torque limitation parameters designed to preserve the structural integrity of the drivetrain.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "800+ hp. The internal forged steel connecting rods, massive crankshaft main journals, and structural cast block are incredibly over-engineered. AMG intentionally limited the factory output to 1000 Nm simply because any higher torque would immediately shear the teeth inside the transfer case differentials.",
