    "timingType": "Double-roller chain (Duplex). The M279 utilizes an incredibly robust and massively thick duplex timing chain setup driven from the front. Because this massive V12 operates at a relatively conservative maximum engine speed from the factory, timing chain stretch or premature structural wear is virtually nonexistent.",
    "injectionType": "Multi-Port Fuel Injection (MPI). Despite being an updated engine, the M279 beautifully retains traditional port fuel injection. This completely eliminates the widespread modern issue of thick carbon sludge accumulation on the intake valves, keeping the massive intake runners pristine for hundreds of thousands of kilometers.",
    "engineLifespan": "250 000 - 400 000+ km. Mechanically, the massive cast iron block and heavy-duty forged components inside the M279 are virtually immortal masterpiece structures. However, its overall operational lifespan is entirely dictated by managing the incredibly high preventative cost of the complex dual ignition coils.",
    "turboType": "Twin-Turbochargers (BiTurbo). Features twin liquid-cooled turbochargers mounted directly to newly redesigned, high-flow exhaust manifolds. They generate an absolute mountain of torque (1000 Nm) instantly from idle, but pack immense thermal density into the tight, unyielding W463 engine compartment layout.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch Motronic ME7.7.1. A highly complex dual-processor engine management controller built specifically to coordinate the entirely new multi-spark ignition system, electronic wastegates, and aggressive torque limitation parameters designed to preserve the structural integrity of the drivetrain.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "800+ hp. The internal forged steel connecting rods, massive crankshaft main journals, and structural cast block are incredibly over-engineered. AMG intentionally limited the factory output to 1000 Nm simply because any higher torque would immediately shear the teeth inside the transfer case differentials.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "24.5 - 29.5 L/100km",
      "highway": "15.5 - 19.0 L/100km",
      "mixed": "19.0 - 23.0 L/100km",
      "sporty": "36.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "10.0L 5W-40 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.5). Meticulous oil compliance is absolutely required; fresh high-viscosity oil is the primary defense against localized heat crystallization within the turbocharger bearing journals.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Catastrophic Ignition Coil Pack Failure: Despite being an updated design over the older M275, the M279 still utilizes two massive, integrated electronic ignition coil rails (one per cylinder bank). Due to immense heat trapped under the engine cover, the internal insulation breaks down, causing severe misfires under load. Replacing a single coil rail costs thousands of euros.",
      "Ignition Voltage Transformer (Ion Module) Faults: The centralized voltage transformer that supplies high-voltage power to the dual coil packs is prone to internal electronic component failure. A failing transformer triggers random multi-cylinder misfires, forcing the engine into Limp Mode.",
      "Turbocharger Oil Feed/Return Line Leaks: The hard metallic oil lines supplying the twin turbochargers feature rubber soft-joint sections. Extreme localized exhaust heat bakes these seals into hard plastic, causing them to crack and weep engine oil heavily down the side of the engine block.",
      "Extreme Engine Bay Heat Soak: Packaging a massive 6.0-liter BiTurbo V12 inside the tightly confined W463 chassis creates an operational oven. Every surrounding component—including plastic vacuum lines, radiator expansion tanks, and wiring harnesses—hardens and cracks prematurely.",
      "Radiator Expansion Tank Cracks: The small plastic coolant overflow and expansion tanks sit directly in the path of the blistering radiant heat radiating from the twin turbochargers. The plastic turns brittle and splits along the molded seams, causing sudden coolant loss.",
      "Engine Mount Collapse: The incredibly heavy physical weight of the V12 block, combined with 1000 Nm of torque, utterly destroys the fluid-filled hydraulic engine mounts rapidly, leading to harsh drivetrain vibrations."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Astronomical Valvetrain Misfire Damage: If the notorious ignition coil pack breakdown is ignored and the vehicle is continuously driven with misfires, raw unburnt fuel will dump into the exhaust stream. This instantly superheats and melts the massive, highly expensive catalytic converters.",
      "Catastrophic Drivetrain Shear: If an aftermarket tuner removes the factory ECU torque limitations without upgrading the mechanical drivetrain, the immediate application of over 1150+ Nm of torque will violently strip the internal gears of the factory transfer case or front differentials.",
      "Hydraulic Fluid Fire Hazard: The power steering and high-pressure chassis fluid lines are routed tightly along the engine block. If a line cracks due to thermal stress and sprays pressurized fluid directly onto the glowing hot turbocharger housings, it presents an immediate fire risk.",
      "Water-to-Air Intercooler Pump Failure: The system utilizes an electric charge cooler pump to circulate coolant through the intercoolers. If this pump dies unnoticed, intake air temperatures skyrocket, causing severe pre-ignition detonation that can damage the pistons.",
      "Oil Pan Gasket Labor Nightmare: The upper and lower oil pan gaskets will eventually develop slow leaks. Because of the massive physical size of the V12 block and the rigid solid-axle front suspension layout, replacing the upper gasket requires completely hoisting the engine out."
    ],
