    "timingType": "Double-roller chain (Duplex). The OM602 utilizes a massively over-engineered duplex timing chain that easily outlasts the vehicle itself. However, the chain is driven in conjunction with the engine vacuum pump; if the vacuum pump bearing collapses, it can drop metallic debris directly into the timing chain track.",
    "injectionType": "Indirect Injection (IDI) via pre-combustion chambers. It uses a purely mechanical Bosch PES inline injection pump. This specific military 'Wolf' iteration is mechanically detuned to tolerate extremely poor fuel quality, high-sulfur diesel, NATO JP-8 jet fuel, or even kerosene without suffering internal damage.",
    "engineLifespan": "500 000 - 1 000 000+ km. Mechanically, the OM602 naturally aspirated diesel is universally regarded as one of the most indestructible internal combustion engines ever created by human hands. The military spec prioritizes absolute survivability in austere environments over all other metrics.",
    "turboType": "Naturally Aspirated. It entirely lacks forced induction. The absence of a turbocharger ensures extremely low internal cylinder pressures and vastly simplifies field repairs, contributing to its million-kilometer military lifespan.",
    "ecuType": "None. The engine is entirely mechanically governed. The military versions often utilize a completely waterproofed 24-Volt electrical architecture (instead of standard 12V) to comply with NATO specifications and allow for deep water fording.",
    "euroStandard": "Pre-Euro",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "150+ hp. The internal forged connecting rods and cast iron block can easily handle immense abuse. However, increasing the power defeats the core military purpose of the 84 hp tune: absolute tolerance of abysmal fuel quality and thermal overload.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "15.0 - 17.5 L/100km",
      "highway": "12.5 - 14.5 L/100km",
      "mixed": "13.5 - 15.5 L/100km",
      "sporty": "17.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "7.0L 15W-40 Heavy Duty Mineral (MIL-SPEC / MB 228.3). This engine is incredibly tolerant of oil quality, but frequent 5,000 km intervals are necessary in harsh, dusty environments to remove heavy diesel soot and particulate ingress.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Vacuum Pump Bearing Failure: The most infamous and potentially fatal flaw. The vacuum pump is driven off the injection pump timer. If its internal roller bearing fails, metal ball bearings fall directly into the timing chain, snapping it and destroying the engine.",
      "24V Component Scarcity: Military models typically utilize a 24-Volt electrical system. Sourcing 24V alternators, 24V starter motors, and 24V glow plug relays is significantly more expensive and difficult than standard 12V civilian components.",
      "Injection Pump Delivery Valve Leaks: The rubber O-rings sealing the delivery valves on top of the Bosch inline injection pump harden over decades. This causes raw diesel to pool on top of the pump, introducing air into the lines and creating hard starting conditions.",
      "Glow Plug Relay Failure (24V): The specialized 24V glow plug relay unit frequently suffers from burnt internal contacts due to heavy field use. If it fails, the engine will refuse to start in cold weather.",
      "Injector Nozzle Wear: The mechanical injector nozzles wear out over hundreds of thousands of kilometers. Because this model is tuned to run on bad fuel, worn nozzles will cause incredibly loud diesel 'nailing' and massive black smoke clouds.",
      "Viscous Fan Clutch Failure: The mechanical fan clutch loses its internal fluid over decades. In slow-moving convoy or off-road situations, failure will cause the massive cast iron engine to overheat, potentially cracking the long aluminum cylinder head."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Catastrophic Timing Chain Derailment: Exclusively caused by the aforementioned vacuum pump bearing failure. Preventative replacement of the vacuum pump with the updated Pierburg unit is absolutely mandatory every 150,000 kilometers.",
      "Cylinder Head Cracking: While the bottom end is indestructible, the long aluminum cylinder head is highly sensitive to overheating. If the cooling system is neglected or the fan clutch fails during heavy load, the head will crack between the valve seats.",
      "Lethargic Highway Merging: With only 84 horsepower pushing a heavy, un-aerodynamic military vehicle, highway driving is genuinely dangerous. The vehicle is effectively a tractor designed for off-road torque, not civilian highway speeds.",
      "Differential Locker Actuator Seizing: If the hydraulic differential lock actuators are not exercised regularly after wading through water or mud, the slave cylinders seize internally and the metal lines rust entirely."
    ],
