    "commonIssues": [
      "Black death injector seal leakage is the most notorious issue affecting the OM612 common rail engine. The copper crush washers sealing the fuel injectors fail, allowing combustion gases and raw fuel to weep into the injector wells. This forms a thick, rock-hard black carbon crust that buries the entire top of the engine head.",
      "High-pressure fuel pump fuel weeping occurs frequently due to continuous pressure cycles. The internal seals inside the mechanical high-pressure pump degrade over time, leading to external diesel leaks directly onto the accessory belt. This softens the rubber belt and can cause sudden belt snap or engine bay contamination.",
      "Intake manifold swirl flap failure compromises the variable induction architecture of this five-cylinder setup. The internal plastic flaps and their corresponding linkages wear down from continuous soot exposure and stick or break. This causes rough idling, severe loss of low-end torque, and triggers persistent check engine lights.",
      "VTG turbocharger actuator sticking restricts the variable vane mechanism inside the exhaust housing. Heavy diesel soot deposits bake onto the internal vanes, preventing smooth geometry adjustment under heavy acceleration. The vehicle will experience sudden boost drops, plunging the engine into an frustrating Limp Mode.",
      "Crankshaft position sensor failure causes completely erratic engine behavior or sudden shutdown while driving. The sensor suffers internal thermal breakdown due to its proximity to the blistering hot transmission bellhousing. Once it fails, the ECU loses the timing signal and prevents the engine from restarting until it cools down.",
      "Fuel rail pressure regulator valve leaks prevent the common rail from maintaining stable operating pressures. The delicate internal micro-seals wear down, leading to erratic fuel pressure fluctuations and rough engine operation. Sufferers will notice extended cranking times or random stalling at stoplights."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Injector hold-down stretch bolt breakage represents a terrifying mechanical risk during routine injector servicing. Mercedes utilized single-use stretch bolts to secure the fuel injectors into the aluminum cylinder head structure. Surcharged with torque, these brittle bolts frequently snap deep inside the head threads if reused or over-torqued.",
      "Cylinder head erosion occurs if the notorious injector blow-by leakage is completely ignored for thousands of kilometers. The escaping superheated combustion gases act like a cutting torch against the aluminum injector seats. This destroys the face of the cylinder head, requiring highly expensive machining or total replacement.",
      "High-pressure fuel rail crack generation can lead to sudden, catastrophic fuel atomization fires under the hood. Operating at immense pressures, any micro-fracture within the steel rail or fuel lines will spray a fine mist of diesel fuel. If this mist contacts the glowing hot exhaust manifold, it will ignite instantly.",
      "Linear piston cracking can occur due to faulty fuel injector spray patterns over prolonged ownership periods. If a common rail injector becomes partially clogged or sticks open, it will create a concentrated fuel jet. This localized thermal stress melts a hole straight through the piston crown.",
      "SAM module water intrusion introduces an absolute electronic nightmare into the vehicle's electrical infrastructure. Sited near the firewall drains, the Signal Acquisition Module can easily become flooded if debris blocks the plastic water drains. This short-circuits the vehicle's electronics, causing random wipers, dead gauges, or complete no-start issues.",
      "Front axle swivel hub seal degradation exposes the delicate front constant velocity joints to heavy trail mud and water. The internal grease washes out completely, leading to rapid bearing wear and severe clunking noises while turning. Rebuilding these heavy solid-axle steering joints requires highly specialized tools and heavy labor."
    ],
