    "timingType": "Simplex timing chain. The OM642 utilizes a single-row timing chain. Due to the high torque of the V6 diesel and prolonged oil change intervals often seen in fleet use, the chain is notoriously prone to stretching. This causes a metallic rattle on cold starts and alters camshaft timing. Preventative replacement around 200,000 km is highly recommended.",
    "injectionType": "Common Rail Direct Injection (CRDI) via precise Piezo injectors operating at up to 1600 bar. This Euro 5 compliant fleet software utilizes more complex post-injection events to lower particulate matter and manage the strict DPF regeneration cycles.",
    "engineLifespan": "300 000 - 500 000+ km. The fundamental aluminum block with cast-iron cylinder liners is extremely robust. The engine's lifespan is entirely dictated by managing its notorious peripheral oil leaks (specifically the oil cooler seals) and preventing timing chain stretch.",
    "turboType": "Variable Geometry Turbocharger (VTG) mounted centrally inside the 'V' of the engine block. Provides excellent low-end torque, but its location makes it highly susceptible to heat soak and oil contamination from the adjacent PCV breather.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch EDC16CP31. A highly complex engine management unit. For this specific 'Green Fleet' Euro 5 tune, it features a bespoke commercial map that slightly bumps torque to 440 Nm over early Professional models, while aggressively managing the DPF and EGR to meet emissions.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "300+ hp. The internal rotating assembly is massively over-engineered. The identical core engine is used in standard passenger cars producing 265 hp and 620 Nm. Removing the 'Professional' software restriction unlocks immense, safe power.",
