    "timingType": "Double-roller chain (Duplex). Located at the absolute rear of the engine block against the firewall. This unconventional layout significantly shortens the overall engine block length, but if the extremely robust chains or tensioners ever fail or stretch at high mileages, it requires a financially devastating engine-out procedure.",
    "injectionType": "Direct Injection (CGI) operating at up to 200 bar via highly precise Piezo injectors. This extreme pressure ensures phenomenal fuel atomization for strict emissions standards, but guarantees thick, tar-like carbon buildup on the intake valves over time since fuel no longer washes over them.",
    "engineLifespan": "200 000 - 350 000+ km. Mechanically, the closed-deck aluminum block and forged internals are virtually indestructible. However, the true lifespan is profoundly dictated by the extreme, relentless heat soak generated by the 'Hot-V' turbocharger layout, which slowly bakes every plastic, rubber, and electronic component under the hood.",
    "turboType": "Twin-Turbochargers (BiTurbo) in a 'Hot-V' configuration. Both turbochargers are mounted entirely inside the 'V' of the cylinder banks rather than on the outside. This completely eliminates turbo lag and provides an instant surge of torque, but generates highly concentrated, extreme temperatures that constantly threaten surrounding components.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch MED17.7.5. An incredibly complex and powerful engine management unit. For the 'Final Edition', Mercedes implemented a significantly more aggressive throttle mapping and software tuning to bump output to a massive 500 hp, celebrating the end of the non-AMG V8 era.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6d",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "600+ hp. The internal forged connecting rods, forged crankshaft, and closed-deck block are identical to the core architecture used in the high-output AMG models. The block handles massive boost effortlessly, with limits dictated entirely by thermal management and the OPF restriction.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "15.0 - 18.0 L/100km",
      "highway": "11.0 - 13.0 L/100km",
      "mixed": "12.0 - 14.5 L/100km",
      "sporty": "22.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "9.0L 5W-40 Fully Synthetic Low-SAPS (MB 229.51 or MB 229.52). Absolute adherence to strict 10,000 km oil change intervals is mandatory to protect the deeply buried, blazing-hot turbochargers and the OPF matrix from ash clogging.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Hot-V Component Baking: The twin turbos sit inside the engine valley, generating colossal radiant heat. Over tens of thousands of kilometers, this heat slowly bakes and destroys the ignition coils, the engine wiring harness, and all surrounding plastic vacuum lines. Preventative replacement of these components is required.",
      "Turbocharger Coolant Line Leaks: The thin cooling lines that supply the 'Hot-V' turbos are subjected to the most extreme thermal stress imaginable. The rubber seals inside the quick-connect fittings harden and fail over time. This causes slow, highly damaging coolant leaks directly onto the hot engine block.",
      "Ignition Coil Failure: Because the ignition coils are mounted dangerously close to the 'Hot-V' exhaust heat, they degrade and crack much faster than on traditional engines. This leads to severe misfires under heavy boost, which can instantly damage the catalytic converters. Regular replacement is considered standard maintenance.",
      "Direct Injection Carbon Buildup: Because fuel is injected directly into the cylinders, it never washes over the intake valves. Thick, tar-like carbon sludge builds up rapidly on the valve stems, causing random misfires and heavily restricting engine airflow. Professional walnut blasting is required to restore performance.",
      "PCV Oil Separator Failure: The complex Positive Crankcase Ventilation system frequently fails due to internal membrane degradation. This allows thick oil vapors to be sucked directly into the turbochargers and intercoolers. This dramatically lowers the fuel's octane rating and heavily accelerates carbon buildup.",
      "OPF/GPF Sensor Failures: European models are equipped with an Otto Particulate Filter (OPF) which monitors exhaust backpressure. The pressure differential sensors frequently fail due to soot and extreme heat, triggering a Limp Mode condition. Driving strictly on short city trips will completely clog the ceramic filter matrix."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Catastrophic Engine Overheating: If the buried turbocharger coolant lines fail significantly and dump the engine's coolant supply rapidly. The immense heat generated by the BiTurbo V8 will instantly warp the aluminum block if the driver does not immediately pull over. It is a fatal vulnerability.",
      "Engine-Out Timing Chain Replacement Cost: Because the timing chain drive is located at the absolute rear of the engine block, access is virtually impossible while the engine is in the car. If the chain stretches at high mileage, mechanics must completely remove the engine and transmission assembly. This turns a standard wear item into a massive labor bill.",
      "Cylinder Scoring from Fuel Dilution: The incredibly precise direct injection system operates at massive 200 bar pressures. If a piezo injector fails and becomes stuck open, raw liquid gasoline washes the lubricating oil off the NANOSLIDE cylinder walls. This lack of lubrication causes immediate, fatal scoring of the engine block.",
      "OPF Thermal Runaway: The Otto Particulate Filter relies on extremely high exhaust temperatures to burn off accumulated soot. If the engine misfires or if the sensors fail, the filter can enter a thermal runaway state, melting the internal structure. Replacing the dual OPF exhaust system is financially devastating.",
      "Transmission Torque Overload: The aggressive 500 hp factory tune places immense, continuous mechanical strain on the 9G-Tronic transmission clutches, especially given the vehicle's massive weight. Bypassing the strict 60,000-kilometer fluid service interval practically guarantees premature clutch pack failure.",
      "Electrical System Voltage Drops: The second-generation W463 relies on a vastly more complex, fully digital electrical architecture compared to its predecessor. A weak battery or failing alternator voltage regulator will cause a cascade of terrifying dashboard error messages. It frequently disables the electric power steering and the ABS systems simultaneously."
    ],
