    "timingType": "Double-roller chain. Early models built before 2008 suffered from a catastrophic metallurgical defect in the timing chain idler gear sprocket. The soft metal teeth would wear away prematurely, causing the chain to stretch and skip. Models from late 2008 onwards received a hardened, updated sprocket that permanently resolved this severe issue.",
    "injectionType": "Sequential Multi-Port Fuel Injection (MPI). The M273 beautifully utilizes traditional port injection, meaning the intake valves are constantly washed with fuel. This completely eliminates the severe carbon buildup problems that plague later direct-injected Mercedes engines.",
    "engineLifespan": "250 000 - 400 000+ km. Mechanically, the all-aluminum block with Silitec cylinder linings is incredibly robust. Assuming the vehicle has the updated idler gear (or is a post-2008 model) and the intake manifold flaps are addressed, the engine easily surpasses 400,000 kilometers with routine maintenance.",
    "turboType": "Naturally Aspirated. The engine relies entirely on atmospheric pressure, providing exceptionally smooth, linear power delivery and instantaneous throttle response without the immense heat soak associated with modern BiTurbo layouts.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch ME9.7. A highly reliable engine management system that precisely controls the dual-stage intake manifold and the continuously variable camshaft timing. The ECU is well-mapped but is sensitive to voltage drops from failing alternators.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 4 / Euro 5",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "450+ hp. The internal forged steel connecting rods and aluminum block are heavily over-engineered. However, since the engine is naturally aspirated, achieving significant power gains requires adding an expensive aftermarket supercharger system.",
