    "timingType": "Simplex timing chain. The OM642 utilizes a single-row timing chain. Due to the massive 540 Nm torque output and prolonged factory oil change intervals, the chain is notoriously prone to stretching. This causes a distinct metallic rattle on cold starts. Preventative replacement around 150,000 - 200,000 km is highly recommended.",
    "injectionType": "Common Rail Direct Injection (CRDI) utilizing Piezo injectors. This specific US-market 'BlueTEC' variant was one of the earliest adopters of the highly complex SCR (Selective Catalytic Reduction) system, injecting AdBlue/DEF directly into the exhaust stream to meet strict EPA Tier 2 Bin 5 standards.",
    "engineLifespan": "250 000 - 400 000+ km. The fundamental aluminum block and cast-iron cylinder liners are robust. However, the true lifespan and daily reliability are heavily compromised by the extreme complexity and frequent failures of the early BlueTEC emissions control system, alongside the notorious OM642 oil cooler leaks.",
    "turboType": "Variable Geometry Turbocharger (VTG) mounted centrally inside the 'V' of the engine block. It provides excellent, lag-free low-end torque. However, its location makes it highly susceptible to heat soak and oil contamination from the adjacent PCV breather.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch EDC16CP31. A highly complex engine management unit adapted for the US market. It finely tunes fuel delivery, EGR, DPF regeneration, and the highly sensitive AdBlue (SCR) dosing system. It frequently places the vehicle into 'countdown' Limp Mode if it detects any minor emissions anomalies.",
    "euroStandard": "US EPA Tier 2 Bin 5 / Euro 5 Equivalent",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "300+ hp. The internal rotating assembly is massively over-engineered. However, pushing power beyond 260 hp is highly discouraged as it rapidly overwhelms the extremely sensitive, highly restrictive early SCR and DPF exhaust components.",
