    "timingType": "Simplex timing chain. The OM642 engine utilizes a single-row timing chain. Due to the massive 510 Nm torque output of the V6 diesel and prolonged factory oil change intervals, the chain is notoriously prone to stretching. This causes a distinct metallic rattle on cold starts. Preventative replacement around 150,000 - 200,000 km is highly recommended to avoid catastrophic valvetrain damage.",
    "injectionType": "Common Rail Direct Injection (CRDI) utilizing highly precise Piezo injectors operating at 1600 bar. This system delivers phenomenally smooth and quiet diesel combustion. The 'BlueEFFICIENCY' moniker introduced aerodynamic tweaks, on-demand power steering, and alternator decoupling to save fuel, but retained the robust OM642.940 injection architecture.",
    "engineLifespan": "300 000 - 450 000+ km. The fundamental aluminum block with cast-iron cylinder liners is exceptionally robust. The engine's lifespan is entirely dictated by managing its notorious peripheral oil leaks (specifically the oil cooler seals), preventing timing chain stretch, and avoiding Limp Mode from failed swirl flap motors.",
    "turboType": "Variable Geometry Turbocharger (VTG) mounted centrally inside the 'V' of the engine block. It provides excellent, lag-free low-end torque. However, its location makes it highly susceptible to heat soak and oil contamination from the adjacent PCV breather.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch EDC16. A highly complex engine management unit that finely tunes fuel delivery, Variable Turbo Geometry, and EGR operation. It is notoriously sensitive to sensor faults, frequently placing the vehicle into Limp Mode if it detects anomalies with the swirl flap motor or exhaust backpressure.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "320+ hp. The internal rotating assembly is massively over-engineered. The identical core block handles aggressive aftermarket tuning effortlessly, with the primary limiting factors being the 7G-Tronic transmission input shaft limits and Exhaust Gas Temperatures (EGTs).",
