    "yearRange": "2010-2012",
    "timingType": "Simplex timing chain. The OM642 utilizes a single-row timing chain. Because this 'LS' (Leistungssteigerung) variant produces a massive 620 Nm of torque, the simplex chain is placed under significantly more mechanical stress than earlier 510 Nm versions. Stretch is highly common; preventative replacement around 150,000 km is absolutely critical to avoid catastrophic valvetrain damage.",
    "injectionType": "Common Rail Direct Injection (CRDI) utilizing highly precise Piezo injectors operating at an increased 1800 bar. This system delivers phenomenally smooth diesel combustion and supports the massive power jump, but the copper crush washers sealing the injectors remain prone to failure ('Black Death').",
    "engineLifespan": "300 000 - 450 000+ km. The fundamental aluminum block with cast-iron cylinder liners is exceptionally robust. As this model generally lacks the hyper-fragile BlueTEC system found in the USA, its lifespan is entirely dictated by managing its notorious oil cooler leaks, preventing timing chain stretch, and maintaining the turbos.",
    "turboType": "Upgraded Variable Geometry Turbocharger (VTG) mounted centrally inside the 'V' of the engine block. The 'LS' variant features a revised, ball-bearing turbo for faster spooling and higher flow. It provides lag-free, massive low-end torque, but remains highly susceptible to heat soak and oil contamination from the adjacent PCV breather.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch EDC17. A highly complex, updated engine management unit that finely tunes the increased 1800-bar fuel delivery, upgraded VTG operation, and EGR. It is notoriously sensitive to sensor faults, frequently placing the vehicle into Limp Mode if it detects anomalies with the swirl flap motor.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "330+ hp. The internal rotating assembly is massively over-engineered. The block handles aggressive aftermarket tuning effortlessly, with the primary limiting factors being the 7G-Tronic Plus transmission input shaft limits and Exhaust Gas Temperatures (EGTs).",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "11.5 - 13.5 L/100km",
      "highway": "8.0 - 9.5 L/100km",
      "mixed": "9.0 - 11.0 L/100km",
      "sporty": "14.5+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "8.0L 5W-30 Premium Fully Synthetic Low-SAPS MB 229.51. Strict 10,000 km intervals are absolutely critical to prevent timing chain stretch under the massive 620 Nm torque load, and to protect the upgraded turbocharger bearings.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Oil Cooler Seal Leak: The absolute most famous and devastating flaw of the OM642. The oil cooler sits deep inside the 'V' of the engine block. The factory orange rubber seals fail due to extreme heat, leaking massive amounts of oil down the back of the engine bellhousing. Requires a massive labor bill to access.",
      "Swirl Flap Motor (M55) Contamination: The red rubber seal connecting the turbocharger intake pipe to the turbo frequently leaks oil vapor. This oil drips directly onto the electronic swirl flap actuator motor situated beneath it, short-circuiting the motor and throwing the vehicle into Limp Mode.",
      "Accelerated Timing Chain Stretch: The massive 620 Nm torque output of the 'LS' variant brutally stretches the single-row simplex timing chain faster than earlier models. Causes a loud rattle on cold start. If ignored, the chain can jump teeth, destroying the valvetrain.",
      "Injector 'Black Death': The copper crush washers sealing the fuel injectors fail, allowing combustion gases and raw fuel to weep into the injector wells. This forms a thick, rock-hard black carbon crust that seizes the injectors into the cylinder head.",
      "Glow Plug Module Failure: The electronic glow plug control module frequently burns out internally, causing hard starting in cold weather and persistent check engine lights. Removing the glow plugs themselves also carries a high risk of snapping them.",
      "Airmatic Suspension Collapse: While technically a chassis flaw, the heavy GL-Class brutally punishes its Airmatic struts and airbags. The rubber bladders dry rot and leak overnight, causing the vehicle to sink to the ground.",
      "7G-Tronic Plus Conductor Plate Issues: While more robust than early 7G units, the transmission control module inside the hot oil pan can still suffer speed sensor failures due to intense heat, locking the transmission into Limp Mode."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Massive Oil Cooler Labor Cost: Replacing the failed orange oil cooler seals costs very little in parts, but demands 10-12 hours of intensive labor to dismantle the turbocharger, fuel lines, and intake manifolds. It is a financially devastating maintenance item.",
      "Valvetrain Destruction: If the cold-start metallic rattle of a stretched timing chain is ignored, the massive torque of the engine will eventually cause the chain to skip teeth, driving the pistons directly into the valves and destroying the engine block.",
      "Limp Mode Paralysis: The updated EDC17 is highly electronic. Any failure of the M55 swirl motor, EGR valve sticking, or exhaust pressure sensor failure will instantly trigger Limp Mode, cutting turbo boost completely and making the heavy GL dangerously slow.",
      "Catastrophic Engine Seizure (Black Death): If the injector blow-by leakage is ignored for thousands of kilometers, the escaping superheated combustion gases act like a cutting torch against the aluminum injector seats, destroying the cylinder head.",
      "Front SAM Water Intrusion: The X164 chassis is notorious for clogged cowl drains near the windshield. Rainwater pools up and overflows directly onto the front Signal Acquisition Module (SAM), destroying the vehicle's electrical nervous system.",
      "Airmatic Compressor Burnout: If a leaking air suspension strut is ignored, the Airmatic air compressor will run continuously to keep the heavy SUV lifted. This rapidly overheats and burns out the expensive compressor motor."
    ],
