    "timingType": "Simplex timing chain. The OM642 utilizes a single-row timing chain. Because this engine produces a massive 617 Nm of torque, the simplex chain is placed under extreme mechanical stress. Stretch is highly common; preventative replacement around 150,000 km is absolutely critical to avoid a cold-start rattle progressing to catastrophic valvetrain damage.",
    "injectionType": "Common Rail Direct Injection (CRDI) utilizing highly precise Piezo injectors. This specific US-market 'BlueTEC' variant features an incredibly restrictive SCR (Selective Catalytic Reduction) system, heavily detuned via software to meet the astronomically strict US EPA Tier 2 Bin 5 and Tier 3 emissions standards.",
    "engineLifespan": "250 000 - 400 000+ km. The fundamental aluminum block and cast-iron cylinder liners are highly robust. However, the daily reliability and true lifespan in North America are severely compromised by the extreme complexity, frequent failures, and crippling dealership repair costs of the early US-spec BlueTEC emissions control system.",
    "turboType": "Upgraded Variable Geometry Turbocharger (VTG) mounted centrally inside the 'V' of the engine block. It provides excellent, lag-free low-end torque. However, its location makes it highly susceptible to heat soak and oil contamination from the adjacent PCV breather.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch EDC17. A highly complex engine management unit adapted specifically for the US market. It finely tunes fuel delivery, EGR, DPF regeneration, and the highly sensitive AdBlue (SCR) dosing system. It frequently places the vehicle into a terrifying 'countdown' Limp Mode if it detects any minor emissions anomalies.",
    "euroStandard": "US EPA Tier 2 Bin 5 / Tier 3",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "330+ hp. The internal rotating assembly is massively over-engineered. However, pushing power beyond 290 hp is highly discouraged as it rapidly overwhelms the extremely sensitive, highly restrictive US-spec SCR and DPF exhaust components.",
