    "turboType": "Single Twin-Scroll Turbocharger (IHI). Very compact and highly responsive, providing maximum 250 Nm torque at just 1,250 RPM. The internal wastegate pivot can wear over time, causing a rattling noise on deceleration and eventual underboost Limp Mode codes.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch MED17.7.2. A highly sophisticated engine management unit. In the GLA 200, this ECU runs the standard hardware at its nominal factory output, providing a very balanced and efficient daily driver experience.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "220+ hp. The internal rotating assembly is heavily over-engineered. The block handles Stage 1 tuning effortlessly, with the primary limiting factors being the physical airflow limits of the small IHI turbocharger and the 7G-DCT dual-clutch transmission's torque capacity.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "7.5 - 8.5 L/100km",
      "highway": "5.0 - 6.0 L/100km",
      "mixed": "5.9 - 7.0 L/100km",
      "sporty": "9.5+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "5.8L 5W-40 Fully Synthetic MB 229.5. Strict 10,000 km intervals are absolutely critical to protect the fragile VVT camshaft adjusters and the timing chain.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Camshaft Adjuster (VVT Phaser) Rattle: The absolute defining flaw of the M270. The internal locking mechanism in the intake and exhaust camshaft adjusters wears out. This causes a terrifying metallic clatter on cold starts. Requires expensive replacement of the adjusters.",
      "Direct Injection Carbon Buildup: Because fuel is injected directly into the cylinders, it never washes over the intake valves. Thick, tar-like carbon sludge builds up rapidly on the valve stems, causing rough idling, misfires, and heavily restricting engine airflow.",
      "Thermostat Failure: The electronically mapped thermostat frequently sticks open. This prevents the engine from reaching its optimal operating temperature, resulting in poor heater performance, increased fuel consumption, and check engine lights.",
      "Coolant Pump Leaks: The water pump features a plastic housing and a vacuum-actuated shroud. The plastic warps from heat cycling, or the internal seals fail, causing coolant to weep down the front of the engine block.",
      "PCV Valve / Oil Separator Failure: The crankcase ventilation system's internal diaphragm tears. This causes excessive oil vapor to be sucked into the intake tract, increasing oil consumption and rapidly accelerating carbon buildup.",
      "High-Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP) Wear: The mechanical HPFP driven by the camshaft degrades over time, leading to extended cranking times, lack of top-end power, and P0087 check engine codes for low rail pressure.",
      "7G-DCT Dual Clutch Judder: The 7-speed dual-clutch transmission can suffer from rough shifts and low-speed juddering. This is often caused by degraded transmission fluid or a failing dual-clutch pack if the vehicle was abused in stop-and-go traffic."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Valvetrain Destruction: If the cold-start metallic rattle of the VVT camshaft adjusters is ignored, the violent chain slap will eventually break the plastic chain guides or cause the chain to skip teeth, resulting in catastrophic piston-to-valve contact.",
      "Intake Valve Seat Burning: If severe carbon buildup on the intake valves is ignored, the valves may fail to seat and seal properly. Hot combustion gases will escape past the valves, burning the edges and requiring a cylinder head rebuild.",
      "Engine Overheating via Plastic Pump Failure: A sudden structural failure of the plastic water pump housing can rapidly dump the engine's coolant supply. If the driver does not notice the temperature gauge, the aluminum block will warp.",
      "Mechatronic Unit Failure (7G-DCT): Skipping the mandatory transmission fluid changes leads to metallic debris circulating through the delicate solenoids of the 7G-DCT mechatronic unit, eventually requiring a highly expensive replacement.",
      "Turbocharger Wastegate Wear: The pivot point on the turbocharger wastegate linkage wears loose. This not only causes an annoying rattle but eventually prevents the wastegate from sealing, causing an underboost Limp Mode.",
      "Oil Dilution from Short Trips: The high-pressure direct injection system can cause fuel blow-by into the oil pan if the car is strictly driven on short city trips where the oil never reaches full operating temperature to evaporate the fuel."
    ],
