    "timingType": "Simplex timing chain located at the REAR of the engine block. The OM651 is infamous for timing chain stretch. Because the chain is located against the firewall, replacing it requires removing the transmission or hoisting the entire engine out of the vehicle, resulting in a terrifying labor bill. A metallic rattle on cold starts is the early warning sign.",
    "injectionType": "Common Rail Direct Injection (CRDI). Early models used failure-prone Delphi Piezo injectors, which were heavily recalled and replaced with magnetic solenoid injectors. Injector copper seal failure ('Black Death') remains a common issue.",
    "engineLifespan": "300 000 - 500 000+ km. The cast-iron block itself is virtually indestructible and heavily utilized in commercial Sprinter vans. The engine's true lifespan is entirely dictated by addressing the rear-mounted timing chain stretch, replacing the plastic water pump, and keeping the DPF clean.",
    "turboType": "Single Variable Geometry Turbocharger (VTG). Unlike the BiTurbo OM651 variants, this 136 hp version relies on a single turbo. Highly reliable but prone to heat soak and DPF backpressure issues.",
    "ecuType": "Delphi / Bosch (depending on year). A highly complex engine management unit. For the GLA 200 CDI, this ECU contains an extreme artificial software restriction. The engine hardware is identical to the 170 hp GLA 220 CDI, making it a perfect candidate for aftermarket tuning.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5 (CDI) / Euro 6 (d)",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "220+ hp. The internal rotating assembly is massively over-engineered. A Stage 1 software flash safely unleashes 170+ hp instantly, bypassing the factory restriction.",
