    "customLightNames": { "headlights": ["headlights"], "taillights": ["taillights1", "taillights2"], "drllight": ["drls1", "drls2"] },
    "author": "Mercedes-Benz GLA-Class 2020 (https://skfb.ly/pwMYS) by ItsDiyor is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/).",
    "popupAnimationName": "",
    "drivingExperience": "Driving the facelifted OM651-powered GLA 220 d is a remarkably torquey, confident, and highly refined experience. The 2.1-liter diesel engine produces an upgraded 177 horsepower, but it is the 350 Nm of torque available low in the rev range that defines the vehicle. It pulls the compact crossover with significant authority, making highway overtaking effortless and city driving punchy. While not as quiet as modern petrol alternatives, the engine delivers a satisfying, deep diesel growl under load. The 7G-DCT shifts rapidly, masking the diesel's narrow powerband, and the 4MATIC system (when equipped) provides incredible all-weather grip. It feels like a very mature, long-distance cruiser that sips fuel.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The 2.1-liter OM651 engine in the GLA 220 d is a legendarily robust cast-iron block burdened by frustrating peripheral flaws. Mechanically, the block itself is virtually indestructible and is heavily utilized in commercial Sprinter vans. This 177 hp variant represents the final, most refined evolution of the OM651 in the MFA platform, offering great power and massive tuning potential. However, the OM651 has a massive, undeniable Achilles' heel: the timing chain is mounted at the REAR of the engine, right against the firewall. When it stretches (and it will, if oil changes are stretched to 25k km), replacing it requires removing the transmission or hoisting the entire engine out, resulting in a terrifying labor bill. Secondly, the plastic water pump is practically guaranteed to leak coolant, requiring an upgrade to an aftermarket metal unit. The intake manifold swirl flap levers will snap, requiring billet aluminum replacements, and the injectors are prone to 'Black Death' carbon leaks. Furthermore, Euro 6 emissions systems are highly sensitive to driving habits. If you buy an OM651, listen to the cold start at the firewall carefully, upgrade the water pump, budget for a Stage 1 tune, and change the oil every 10,000 km. It is a brilliant, commercial-grade engine hiding in a premium crossover."
