    "drivingExperience": "Driving the facelifted OM654-powered GLB 180 d in its factory 116 horsepower form is an exercise in extreme patience and potential highway anxiety. The GLB is a large, boxy SUV that can seat seven people. Pairing that immense mass and aerodynamic drag with only 116 horsepower means the vehicle is incredibly sluggish off the line, and highway overtaking requires massive pre-planning and a wide-open road. The 280 Nm of torque helps slightly in low-speed city driving, but the engine runs out of breath instantly. The 8G-DCT works overtime, slipping clutches and constantly downshifting just to maintain momentum. The true magic of this car, however, is its tuning potential. A simple Stage 1 software flash instantly unlocks ~190 hp, transforming this painfully slow, overworked crossover into a genuinely rapid, highly capable machine.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The 2.0-liter OM654q engine in the facelifted GLB 180 d is a technological masterpiece wrapped in a web of terrifying complexity, artificially choked by software, and placed in the wrong chassis. Let's start with the incredible hardware: Mercedes combined a lightweight aluminum block with ultra-strong steel pistons and NANOSLIDE coating. The core block is practically indestructible. The secret to the '180 d' is that it is physically identical to the 150 hp and 190 hp variants; the 116 hp limit is pure software to hit strict Euro 6e fleet emission targets. Do yourself a favor: if you buy this heavy SUV, get a Stage 1 tune immediately. Running 116 hp in a GLB forces the transmission and turbo to work at 100% capacity constantly. However, the OM654 has two massive Achilles' heels. First, the timing chain is mounted at the REAR of the engine, right against the firewall. Replacing a stretched chain requires removing the transmission. Second is the infamous water pump vacuum leak. The internal seal fails, sucking coolant into the vacuum lines, destroying the brake booster—a catastrophic repair. You MUST proactively upgrade the water pump and install a vacuum check-valve. Finally, the Euro 6e emissions system is highly sensitive; NOx sensor failures will trigger a dashboard countdown that completely immobilizes the car. Buy it, fix the water pump, tune it to 190hp, change the oil every 10k km, and prepare for AdBlue gremlins."
