    "drivingExperience": "Driving the OM654-powered GLB 200 d is an incredibly balanced and torquey experience, representing the 'sweet spot' of the GLB diesel lineup. The 2.0-liter engine produces 150 horsepower and a healthy 320 Nm of torque. While 150 hp doesn't sound like much for a 7-seater SUV, the massive low-end torque makes city driving genuinely punchy, and highway cruising is perfectly adequate. The engine itself is an acoustic marvel for a four-cylinder diesel; thanks to advanced injection strategies and dual balance shafts, it is whisper-quiet in the cabin. The 8G-DCT shifts seamlessly, masking the diesel's narrow powerband. Furthermore, a simple Stage 1 software flash instantly unlocks ~195 hp, transforming this highly efficient crossover into a genuinely rapid, effortless machine.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The 2.0-liter OM654q engine in the GLB 200 d is a technological masterpiece wrapped in a web of terrifying complexity and a few fatal design flaws. Let's start with the incredible hardware: Mercedes combined a lightweight aluminum block with ultra-strong steel pistons (which expand less under heat) and NANOSLIDE coating. The core block is practically indestructible and is heavily utilized in commercial Sprinter vans. The secret to the '200 d' is that it utilizes the exact same 2.0L hardware as the 190 hp '220 d' variant; the 150 hp limit is pure software, making it a highly lucrative tuning platform to move the heavy GLB chassis easily. However, the OM654 has two massive, undeniable Achilles' heels. First, the timing chain is mounted at the REAR of the engine, right against the firewall. When it stretches (which happens if oil changes are pushed to 25k km), replacing it requires removing the transmission, resulting in a terrifying labor bill. Second is the infamous water pump vacuum leak. The water pump's internal seal fails, allowing coolant to be sucked into the engine's vacuum lines, traveling to and destroying the brake booster—a catastrophic repair. You MUST proactively upgrade the water pump and install a vacuum check-valve. Finally, the Euro 6d emissions system is highly sensitive; NOx sensor failures will trigger a dashboard countdown that completely immobilizes the car. If you buy an OM654, proactively fix the water pump, budget for a Stage 1 tune, and change the oil every 10,000 km."
