    "yearRange": "2012-2015",
    "timingType": "Duplex timing chain (V6 at 60-degree angle). The M276 suffers from a notorious flaw with its secondary timing chain tensioners and lack of check valves. This allows oil to drain back overnight, causing a terrifying metal-on-metal rattle on cold starts before oil pressure builds.",
    "injectionType": "Direct Injection (CGI) operating at up to 200 Bar via advanced Piezo injectors. While highly efficient, this system introduces significant intake valve carbon buildup and is extremely sensitive to poor fuel quality.",
    "engineLifespan": "250 000 - 350 000+ km. The 60-degree aluminum block is incredibly sturdy. Lifespan is heavily dictated by proactively installing the revised timing chain tensioners and check valves, as well as replacing the high-pressure fuel pump when it wears.",
    "turboType": "Naturally Aspirated. Uses a variable resonance intake manifold to maximize airflow across the RPM range.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch MED17.7.3. A highly advanced direct-injection ECU. In the GLK 300, it is used to artificially choke the 3.5L engine down to 252 hp (the GLK 350 makes 306 hp with identical hardware).",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "330+ hp. The bottom end is forged and practically identical to the 306 hp variant. The 252 hp output is a pure software limitation for tax/market placement.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "11.0 - 12.5 L/100km",
      "highway": "7.5 - 8.8 L/100km",
      "mixed": "8.8 - 10.0 L/100km",
      "sporty": "14.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "6.5L 5W-30 or 5W-40 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.5).",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Cold Start Timing Chain Rattle: The definitive M276 issue. Oil drains out of the secondary tensioners overnight. Upon starting, the slack chains slap the guides for 2-4 seconds until pressure builds. Requires revised tensioners and check valves.",
      "High-Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP) Failure: The camshaft-driven HPFP wears internally, causing long cranks, lack of power, and an audible ticking noise.",
      "Direct Injection Carbon Buildup: Lack of port injection means oil vapors bake onto the intake valves, severely restricting airflow and causing rough idle after 80,000 km.",
      "NOX Sensor Failure: Engines operating in stratified charge mode burn very lean, causing high NOX emissions. The sensitive NOX sensors fail frequently, triggering a Check Engine Light.",
      "PCV / Oil Separator Whine: The crankcase breather diaphragm tears, creating a massive vacuum leak, high oil consumption, and a loud squealing noise at idle.",
      "Coolant Pump / Thermostat Leaks: The plastic housing of the electronic thermostat cracks over time, while the mechanical water pump bearings can develop play.",
      "Piezo Injector Failure: Highly sensitive injectors can fail open, dumping fuel into the cylinder, washing the oil film, and risking catastrophic cylinder scoring."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Catastrophic Valvetrain Destruction: If the cold start tensioner rattle is ignored for tens of thousands of kilometers, the chain will eventually stretch, skip teeth, and cause the pistons to smash the valves.",
      "HPFP Fuel Dilution: A severely failed high-pressure fuel pump can leak raw gasoline directly into the engine crankcase, diluting the oil and destroying all engine bearings instantly.",
      "Cylinder Scoring (Injector Failure): A failed Piezo injector sticking open acts like a pressure washer, stripping the vital oil film off the cylinder wall and causing terminal scoring.",
      "Conductor Plate Failure (7G-Tronic Plus): If transmission fluid is neglected, metallic debris destroys the delicate speed sensors inside the transmission conductor plate.",
      "ECU Coolant Intrusion: A failed electronic thermostat can sometimes push coolant through the wiring harness via capillary action, eventually reaching and frying the ECU.",
      "Catalyst Destruction: Driving with misfires caused by carbon buildup or failing coils will superheat and melt the expensive catalytic converters.",
      "Transfer Case Bearing Failure: The 4MATIC transfer case bearings are prone to pitting if the fluid is not changed strictly at 60k km, requiring a very costly teardown."
    ],
