    "timingType": "Simplex timing chain located at the REAR of the engine block. This controversial design remains unchanged in the facelift. Placing the chain against the firewall means that if it stretches—which occurs if oil changes are neglected—replacing it requires dropping the transmission or removing the engine.",
    "injectionType": "Common Rail Direct Injection (CRDI). Utilizes highly reliable Bosch solenoid injectors (operating at up to 2000 Bar), avoiding the catastrophic Piezo injector failures that plagued the higher-tier OM651 engines.",
    "engineLifespan": "350 000 - 500 000+ km. The OM651 is legendary for its commercial 'taxi-cab' durability. Its lifespan is determined almost entirely by proactively managing the rear-mounted timing chain and bypassing the variable oil pump solenoid.",
    "turboType": "Single Variable Geometry Turbocharger (VTG). Unlike the higher-output twin-turbo OM651 variants, the 200 CDI uses a single, highly durable turbo. It is highly reliable but severely limits top-end passing power in the heavy GLK.",
    "ecuType": "Delphi / Bosch EDC17. A robust engine management system updated to Euro 5+ standards. It artificially chokes the engine to 143 hp to fit fleet and tax regulations, leaving massive, safe headroom for tuning.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5+",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "220+ hp. The internal rotating assembly is forged, massively over-engineered, and identical to the higher-output 220 CDI. The bottom end is completely unstressed at the factory 143 hp level.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "6.8 - 7.8 L/100km",
      "highway": "5.2 - 6.2 L/100km",
      "mixed": "5.8 - 6.8 L/100km",
      "sporty": "8.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "6.5L 5W-30 Fully Synthetic Low-SAPS (MB 229.51).",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Rear Timing Chain Stretch: Extended 25,000 km oil intervals cause the single-row rear timing chain to stretch. A rattling sound on cold starts is the first and only warning.",
      "Variable Oil Pump Solenoid Failure: The OM651 features an eco-focused variable oil pump. The control solenoid can stick, causing low oil pressure at critical RPMs, leading to spun main bearings.",
      "Water Pump & Coolant Flange Leaks: The plastic water pump housing and the coolant connection pipe located under the fuel filter frequently crack and leak coolant slowly.",
      "Intake Manifold Swirl Flap Failure: Plastic linkages on the intake manifold wear out and break, causing boost leaks, terrible fuel economy, and Limp Mode.",
      "DPF Blockage: The heavy GLK chassis forces the low-power 143 hp engine to work very hard. If driven primarily in the city, the DPF will choke with soot rapidly.",
      "EGR Valve Sticking: Soot from the exhaust gas recirculates and solidifies in the EGR valve, causing it to stick open and choke the engine of fresh air.",
      "Fuel Injector Leak-off (Black Death): While rare on later models, copper seals under the injectors can fail, allowing combustion gases to escape and bake into a hard, black tar."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Catastrophic Engine Seizure (Spun Bearings): If the variable oil pump solenoid fails in the 'low pressure' state during high engine load, the crankshaft main bearings will spin, destroying the engine block completely.",
      "Timing Chain Jump / Valvetrain Destruction: Ignoring the cold-start chain rattle will eventually cause the stretched chain to skip a tooth on the camshaft sprockets, resulting in piston-to-valve contact and terminal engine damage.",
      "Engine-Out Maintenance Bill: Because the timing chain is mounted flush against the firewall, replacing it or the chain tensioners requires dropping the transmission or pulling the engine, resulting in an astronomical labor bill.",
      "Turbocharger Bearing Failure: Chronic DPF blockage creates massive exhaust backpressure, which prevents the turbocharger from spooling freely and quickly destroys the delicate VTG turbo bearings.",
      "Hydrolock via EGR Cooler Leak: The internal core of the EGR cooler can crack from thermal stress, allowing engine coolant to leak directly into the intake tract and potentially hydrolock the engine.",
      "Conductor Plate Failure (7G-Tronic Plus): If the automatic transmission is selected and fluid changes are neglected, metallic debris will short out the speed sensors inside the transmission conductor plate."
    ],
