    "timingType": "Simplex timing chain located at the REAR of the engine block. This controversial design remains unchanged in the facelift. Placing the chain against the firewall means that if it stretches—which occurs if oil changes are neglected—replacing it requires dropping the transmission or removing the engine.",
    "injectionType": "Common Rail Direct Injection (CRDI). Crucially, by 2012, Mercedes had completely abandoned the disastrous Delphi Piezo injectors. Facelift models use highly reliable Delphi solenoid injectors from the factory, entirely eliminating the catastrophic sudden-death injector failures of the pre-facelift era.",
    "engineLifespan": "350 000 - 500 000+ km. The OM651 is legendary for its commercial 'taxi-cab' durability. Because the factory injector issues were resolved in this generation, lifespan is determined almost entirely by proactively managing the rear-mounted timing chain and bypassing the variable oil pump solenoid.",
    "turboType": "Sequential Twin-Turbocharger (BorgWarner R2S). Uses a small high-pressure turbo for instant low-end response and a larger low-pressure turbo for mid-to-high RPM power. Provides an incredibly flat torque curve from 1400 RPM.",
    "ecuType": "Delphi / Bosch EDC17. Updated to comply with Euro 5+ emissions. A highly complex system managing sequential turbo transitions and stricter DPF regenerations.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5+",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "240+ hp. The internal rotating assembly is massively over-engineered. The 170 hp factory output barely stresses the hardware, leaving massive, safe headroom for aftermarket tuning.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "7.2 - 8.2 L/100km",
      "highway": "5.6 - 6.6 L/100km",
      "mixed": "6.2 - 7.2 L/100km",
      "sporty": "8.5+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "6.5L 5W-30 Fully Synthetic Low-SAPS (MB 229.51).",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Rear Timing Chain Stretch: Extended 25,000 km oil intervals cause the single-row rear timing chain to stretch. A rattling sound on cold starts is the first warning sign.",
      "Variable Oil Pump Solenoid Failure: The OM651 features an eco-focused variable oil pump. The control solenoid can stick, causing low oil pressure at critical RPMs, leading to spun main bearings.",
      "Water Pump & Coolant Flange Leaks: The plastic water pump housing and the coolant connection pipe located under the fuel filter frequently crack and leak coolant slowly.",
      "Intake Manifold Swirl Flap Failure: Plastic linkages on the intake manifold wear out and break, causing boost leaks, terrible fuel economy, and Limp Mode.",
      "Sequential Turbo Vacuum Leaks: The vacuum transducers controlling the bypass flaps between the small and large turbos frequently fail or leak, causing erratic boost delivery.",
      "DPF Blockage: If driven primarily in the city, the DPF will choke with soot rapidly.",
      "EGR Valve Sticking: Soot from the exhaust gas recirculates and solidifies in the EGR valve, causing it to stick open and choke the engine of fresh air."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Catastrophic Engine Seizure (Spun Bearings): If the variable oil pump solenoid fails in the 'low pressure' state during high engine load, the crankshaft main bearings will spin, destroying the engine block completely.",
      "Timing Chain Jump / Valvetrain Destruction: Ignoring the cold-start chain rattle will eventually cause the stretched chain to skip a tooth on the camshaft sprockets, resulting in piston-to-valve contact and terminal engine damage.",
      "Engine-Out Maintenance Bill: Because the timing chain is mounted flush against the firewall, replacing it requires dropping the transmission or pulling the engine, resulting in an astronomical labor bill.",
      "Turbocharger Bearing Failure: Chronic DPF blockage creates massive exhaust backpressure, which prevents the twin turbos from spooling freely and quickly destroys their bearings.",
      "Hydrolock via EGR Cooler Leak: The internal core of the EGR cooler can crack from thermal stress, allowing engine coolant to leak directly into the intake tract and potentially hydrolock the engine.",
      "Conductor Plate Failure (7G-Tronic Plus): If the automatic transmission fluid is neglected, metallic debris will short out the speed sensors inside the transmission conductor plate."
    ],
