    "timingType": "Simplex timing chain located at the REAR of the engine block. A fundamentally compromised design choice by Mercedes. If the chain stretches due to extended 25,000 km oil intervals, replacing the chain and guides requires dropping the transmission or physically pulling the engine out of the chassis.",
    "injectionType": "Common Rail Direct Injection (CRDI). Early pre-facelift models (2009-2011) were notoriously fitted with disastrous Delphi Piezo injectors that failed globally. Ensure the massive Mercedes recall was performed to retrofit them with Delphi solenoid injectors. Facelift models (2012+) use reliable solenoids from the factory.",
    "engineLifespan": "350 000 - 500 000+ km. The iron block and forged internals are virtually indestructible. Lifespan depends entirely on verifying the Piezo injector recall was completed (on early models), managing the rear timing chain stretch, and bypassing the variable oil pump solenoid.",
    "turboType": "Sequential Twin-Turbocharger (BorgWarner R2S). Uses a small high-pressure turbo for instant low-end response and a larger low-pressure turbo for mid-to-high RPM power. Eliminates turbo lag completely, providing an incredibly flat torque curve.",
    "ecuType": "Delphi / Bosch EDC17. A highly complex system managing sequential turbo transitions and DPF regenerations. In the Fleet Edition, it artificially limits output to 163 hp to exploit tax loopholes in specific European markets (like Belgium).",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "240+ hp. The internal rotating assembly is massively over-engineered. The artificially restricted 163 hp factory output leaves an absolutely massive, safe headroom for aftermarket tuning.",
