    "timingType": "Simplex timing chain located at the REAR of the engine block. A fundamentally compromised design choice by Mercedes. If the chain stretches due to extended 25,000 km oil intervals, replacing the chain and guides requires dropping the transmission or physically pulling the entire engine out of the chassis.",
    "injectionType": "Common Rail Direct Injection (CRDI). As the top-tier 4-cylinder, early pre-facelift 250 CDIs were notoriously fitted with Delphi Piezo injectors. These injectors failed on a massive, catastrophic scale globally, prompting a massive Mercedes recall to retrofit them with Delphi solenoid injectors and a new engine harness. Always verify this recall was performed.",
    "engineLifespan": "350 000 - 500 000+ km. The iron block and forged internals are incredibly overbuilt. Lifespan depends entirely on verifying the Piezo injector recall was completed, managing the rear timing chain stretch, and bypassing the variable oil pump solenoid.",
    "turboType": "Sequential Twin-Turbocharger (BorgWarner R2S). Uses a small high-pressure turbo for instant low-end response and a larger low-pressure turbo for mid-to-high RPM power. Eliminates turbo lag completely, providing an incredible 500 Nm of torque from just 1600 RPM.",
    "ecuType": "Delphi / Bosch EDC17. A highly complex system managing sequential turbo transitions and DPF regenerations. If the Piezo recall was done, the ECU firmware and often the module itself were updated by Mercedes to support solenoid injectors.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "280+ hp. The internal rotating assembly is massively over-engineered to handle the insane low-end torque. The 204 hp factory output is extremely reliable, leaving strong headroom for aftermarket tuning.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "7.8 - 8.8 L/100km",
      "highway": "6.0 - 7.0 L/100km",
      "mixed": "6.8 - 7.8 L/100km",
      "sporty": "9.5+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "6.5L 5W-30 Fully Synthetic Low-SAPS (MB 229.51).",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Delphi Piezo Injector Failure (If not recalled): The original Piezo injectors short-circuited internally, causing sudden engine death on the highway and blowing the ECU.",
      "Rear Timing Chain Stretch: Extended oil intervals cause the single-row rear timing chain to stretch. A rattling sound on cold starts is the first warning sign.",
      "Variable Oil Pump Solenoid Failure: The eco-focused variable oil pump control solenoid can stick, causing dangerously low oil pressure under load, leading to spun main bearings.",
      "Water Pump & Coolant Flange Leaks: The plastic water pump housing and the coolant connection pipe located under the fuel filter frequently crack and leak coolant slowly.",
      "Intake Manifold Swirl Flap Failure: Plastic linkages on the intake manifold wear out and break, causing boost leaks, terrible fuel economy, and Limp Mode.",
      "Sequential Turbo Vacuum Leaks: The vacuum transducers controlling the bypass flaps between the small and large turbos frequently fail or leak, causing erratic boost delivery and Limp Mode.",
      "EGR Valve Sticking: Soot from the exhaust gas recirculates and solidifies in the EGR valve, causing it to stick open and choke the engine of fresh air."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Catastrophic Engine Seizure (Spun Bearings): If the variable oil pump solenoid fails in the 'low pressure' state during high engine load, the crankshaft main bearings will spin, destroying the engine block completely.",
      "Timing Chain Jump / Valvetrain Destruction: Ignoring the cold-start chain rattle will eventually cause the stretched chain to skip a tooth on the camshaft sprockets, resulting in piston-to-valve contact.",
      "Engine-Out Maintenance Bill: Because the timing chain is mounted flush against the firewall, replacing it requires dropping the transmission or pulling the engine, resulting in an astronomical labor bill.",
      "Turbocharger Bearing Failure: Chronic DPF blockage creates massive exhaust backpressure, which prevents the twin turbos from spooling freely and quickly destroys their bearings.",
      "Hydrolock via EGR Cooler Leak: The internal core of the EGR cooler can crack from thermal stress, allowing engine coolant to leak directly into the intake tract and potentially hydrolock the engine.",
      "Conductor Plate Failure (7G-Tronic): If the automatic transmission fluid is neglected, the immense 500 Nm of torque combined with metallic debris will destroy the delicate speed sensors inside the transmission conductor plate."
    ],
