    "riskRating": 5,
    "drivingExperience": "Driving the GLC 200 in its factory 184 horsepower form is an exercise in extreme smoothness, but noticeable sluggishness. The X253 chassis is heavy, and pairing it with an artificially restricted 2.0L engine means the car lacks any real overtaking punch on the highway. However, the true star of the show is the 9G-Tronic transmission. It shifts imperceptibly in the background, masking the engine's power deficit by keeping it strictly in the torque band. The cabin is incredibly quiet, and the suspension absorbs bumps gracefully. It is a fantastic, comfortable daily driver, but it begs for a Stage 1 tune to unlock the 245 horsepower it was physically built to deliver.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The M274.920 inside the GLC 200 is a brilliant piece of modern engine design hampered by three very specific, highly destructive flaws. From a performance standpoint, buying the GLC 200 is a fantastic financial hack: it is physically identical to the more expensive GLC 250, meaning a simple ECU flash safely unlocks 245 horsepower. However, as an owner, your absolute priority is monitoring the intake camshaft adjuster (VVT phaser). If you hear a loud, metallic rattle for 2-3 seconds on a cold start, the phaser's internal locking pin has broken. If you ignore this noise, the timing chain will eventually stretch or jump, causing catastrophic piston-to-valve contact. You MUST replace the phaser immediately upon hearing the rattle. Second, you must abandon Mercedes' recommended 25,000 km oil change intervals. Change the oil strictly every 10,000 km to protect the VVT system and the delicate turbocharger wastegate arm. Finally, watch out for plastic thermostat housing leaks and torn PCV valves. The 9G-Tronic transmission is phenomenal, but its plastic pan (which contains the filter) must be changed along with the fluid every 80,000 km. At a 5/10 risk rating, it's a very manageable luxury vehicle, but only if you are deeply proactive about the cold-start rattle.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "800 - 1,500 EUR / year",
