    "author": ".",
    "riskRating": 7,
    "drivingExperience": "Driving the facelifted GLC 200 with the M264 mild-hybrid system is an astonishingly smooth experience. The 48V EQ Boost starter-generator provides 14 hp of instant electric torque the moment you touch the pedal, completely filling the gap before the turbocharger spools. This makes the heavy SUV feel much lighter and more eager in city traffic than the numbers suggest. The engine stop-start system is completely imperceptible—the engine shuts off while gliding to a stop and restarts silently without the shudder of a traditional starter motor. However, at highway speeds, the artificial 197 hp software restriction becomes very obvious, and the car runs out of breath quickly during high-speed overtaking. The 9G-Tronic shifts beautifully, making it an incredible daily driver that simply begs for a GLC 300 software unlock.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The M264 inside the facelifted GLC 200 is a double-edged sword. On one hand, Mercedes heavily revised the internal engine block, adding CONICSHAPE cylinders and CAMTRONIC valve lift, making it significantly smoother and mechanically more robust than the older M274. Furthermore, because it is physically identical to the 258 hp GLC 300, a simple ECU tune safely unlocks massive power. On the other hand, the 48V EQ Boost mild-hybrid system is a terrifying financial liability. The 48V lithium-ion battery and the Belt-Driven Starter Generator (RSG) fail frequently. When they do, the car completely bricks itself—it will not start, cannot be jump-started, and requires thousands of Euros to fix at a dealership. Additionally, it still suffers from classic Mercedes plastic peripheral failures: the thermostat housings leak, and the PCV systems fail. If you buy one under warranty, it is a technological marvel. If you buy one out of warranty, you must keep a massive emergency repair fund ready for the day the 48V system dies. Due to the catastrophic cost of electrical failures, it earns a high 7/10 risk rating.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "1,200 - 2,500 EUR / year",
