    "author": ".",
    "riskRating": 6,
    "drivingExperience": "Driving the pre-facelift GLC 300 4MATIC offers genuine performance. With 245 horsepower and 370 Nm of torque, it sheds the sluggishness of the lower models and pulls aggressively to highway speeds. The turbocharger spools fast, and the 9G-Tronic transmission provides seamless downshifts when you demand power. The standard 4MATIC all-wheel-drive system ensures the 370 Nm is put down to the road without drama, making it a very capable, quick, and highly refined premium SUV.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The GLC 300 is the most powerful 4-cylinder pre-facelift model, meaning it extracts the absolute maximum from the M274 engine block. While it is fast, it comes with a slightly elevated risk profile (6/10). Because it runs higher boost and more aggressive timing than the GLC 200 or 250, the notorious M274 flaws are subjected to higher thermal and mechanical stress. The piston wrist pins are working much closer to their limits. Therefore, your absolute priority is the intake camshaft adjuster (VVT phaser). If you hear a loud, metallic rattle for 2-3 seconds on a cold start, the phaser is failing and must be replaced immediately. If ignored, the timing chain jumps, and the engine destroys itself. You must use high-quality 5W-40 oil (not 5W-30) and change it strictly every 10,000 km to protect the stressed wrist pins and turbo bearings. If you keep up with proactive maintenance and address thermostat leaks early, it is a fantastic, fast SUV.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "800 - 1,800 EUR / year",
    "model3DHotspots": [
      {
        "label": "Camshaft Adjusters and Timing Chain (Front VVT Area)",
        "x": 0.0,
        "y": 0.06,
        "z": 0.45,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Front of the engine under the hood. The M274's most notorious failure point: intake VVT phaser locking pins break, causing a loud cold-start metallic rattle and potential timing chain jump."
      },
      {
        "label": "High-Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP)",
        "x": -0.08,
        "y": 0.06,
        "z": 0.38,
        "critical": false,
        "details": "Located on top of the engine near the front camshaft drive. Prone to internal wear, leading to extended cranking times and sudden pressure drops under heavy acceleration."
      },
      {
        "label": "Electronic Thermostat Housing",
        "x": 0.12,
        "y": 0.04,
        "z": 0.35,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Side/front of the engine block. The plastic thermostat housing frequently cracks, leaks coolant, or sticks open, causing the engine to run dangerously cold."
      },
      {
        "label": "PCV Valve and Oil Separator",
        "x": 0.0,
        "y": 0.08,
        "z": 0.30,
        "critical": false,
        "details": "Rear section of the valve cover. The internal rubber diaphragm frequently tears, causing high crankcase pressure, oil consumption, and a loud whistling noise at idle."
      },
      {
        "label": "Turbocharger Wastegate Actuator",
        "x": -0.12,
        "y": 0.02,
        "z": 0.32,
        "critical": false,
        "details": "Mounted directly on the turbocharger unit. The electronic actuator arm can seize due to high radiant heat, frequently throwing the vehicle into Limp Mode."
      },
      {
        "label": "9G-Tronic Transmission Integrated Filter Pan",
        "x": 0.0,
        "y": -0.15,
        "z": 0.0,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Underbody midsection. The 9G-Tronic transmission filter is molded directly into the plastic pan and must be replaced along with fluid every 80,000 km."
      }
    ]
