    "author": ".",
    "riskRating": 7,
    "drivingExperience": "Driving the facelifted GLC 300 4MATIC with the M264 mild-hybrid system is genuinely fast and remarkably smooth. The 258 horsepower engine works in perfect harmony with the 48V EQ Boost starter-generator, which provides 14 hp of instant electric torque. This completely masks any turbo lag, giving the heavy SUV instant, aggressive off-the-line acceleration. The engine stop-start system is completely imperceptible—the engine shuts off while gliding to a stop and restarts silently. With 258 hp on tap, highway overtakes are effortless, and the 9G-Tronic shifts beautifully, making it an incredible, high-tech daily driver.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The M264 inside the facelifted GLC 300 is a double-edged sword. On one hand, Mercedes heavily revised the internal engine block, adding CONICSHAPE cylinders and CAMTRONIC valve lift, making it significantly smoother and mechanically more robust than the older M274. On the other hand, the 48V EQ Boost mild-hybrid system is a terrifying financial liability. The 48V lithium-ion battery and the Belt-Driven Starter Generator (RSG) fail frequently. When they do, the car completely bricks itself—it will not start, cannot be jump-started, and requires thousands of Euros to fix at a dealership. Additionally, it still suffers from classic Mercedes plastic peripheral failures: the thermostat housings leak, and the PCV systems fail. If you buy one under warranty, it is a technological marvel that fixes the mechanical flaws of the M274. If you buy one out of warranty, you must keep a massive emergency repair fund ready for the day the 48V system dies. Due to the catastrophic cost of electrical failures, it earns a high 7/10 risk rating.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "1,200 - 2,500 EUR / year",
    "model3DHotspots": [
      {
        "label": "Camshaft Adjusters and Timing Chain",
        "x": 0.0,
        "y": 0.06,
        "z": 0.45,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Front of the engine. While improved over the M274, the timing chain and phasers are still prone to wear if oil changes are neglected."
      },
      {
        "label": "High-Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP)",
        "x": -0.08,
        "y": 0.06,
        "z": 0.38,
        "critical": false,
        "details": "Located on top of the engine near the front camshaft drive. Prone to internal wear, leading to extended cranking times."
      },
      {
        "label": "Electronic Thermostat Housing",
        "x": 0.12,
        "y": 0.04,
        "z": 0.35,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Side/front of the engine block. The plastic thermostat housing frequently cracks, leaks coolant, or sticks open."
      },
      {
        "label": "PCV Valve and Oil Separator",
        "x": 0.0,
        "y": 0.08,
        "z": 0.30,
        "critical": false,
        "details": "Rear section of the valve cover. The internal rubber diaphragm frequently tears, causing high crankcase pressure."
      },
      {
        "label": "48V Belt-Driven Starter Generator (RSG)",
        "x": -0.15,
        "y": 0.02,
        "z": 0.38,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Front side of the engine. The core of the EQ Boost system. If this unit or the 48V battery fails, the vehicle electronically bricks itself and will not start."
      },
      {
        "label": "9G-Tronic Transmission Integrated Filter Pan",
        "x": 0.0,
        "y": -0.15,
        "z": 0.0,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Underbody midsection. The 9G-Tronic transmission filter is molded directly into the plastic pan and must be replaced along with fluid every 80,000 km."
      }
    ]
