    "author": ".",
    "riskRating": 8,
    "drivingExperience": "When fully charged, the GLC 350 e is wonderfully quiet, utilizing its 85 kW electric motor for smooth, emissions-free urban driving. When you press the throttle hard, the 2.0L turbocharged M274 engine fires up, delivering a combined 320 hp and 560 Nm of torque. The acceleration is very brisk, completely masking any turbo lag thanks to the electric torque. However, the early-generation hybrid software shows its age: the transition between electric and petrol power can sometimes be jerky, and the brake pedal feel is notoriously inconsistent due to the blending of regenerative and friction braking. Furthermore, the 8.7 kWh battery provides a realistic EV range of only 20-25 km, forcing the ICE to run frequently.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The pre-facelift GLC 350 e combines the worst of both worlds for out-of-warranty ownership. Firstly, the internal combustion engine is the infamous M274. You must remain vigilant for the catastrophic intake VVT cold-start rattle and plastic thermostat leaks. Furthermore, because it's a PHEV, the engine suffers immense thermal shock—regularly firing up to 3,000 RPM while completely cold. Strict 10,000 km oil changes with 5W-40 are absolutely vital to prevent internal scoring. Secondly, you have a highly complex, early-generation high-voltage system. The 8.7 kWh batteries degrade heavily over 6-8 years, and replacing them costs an exorbitant amount. Additionally, failures of the high-voltage PTC cabin heater and the hybrid inverter are common and very expensive. Due to the high depreciation and massive repair liabilities, it earns a brutal 8/10 risk rating.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "1,500 - 3,000 EUR / year",
