    "riskRating": 8,
    "drivingExperience": "The GLC 300 e offers astonishing performance. With a larger 13.5 kWh battery compared to the older 350e, it provides a much more usable 35-45 km of pure electric range, allowing for entirely silent, emissions-free daily commuting. However, when you floor the throttle, the system unleashes a monstrous 700 Nm of combined torque. The transition from EV to petrol power via the 9G-Tronic transmission is vastly smoother than the older 7-speed hybrid, resulting in violent, immediate acceleration that pushes you deep into the seat, making it significantly faster than the standard GLC 300.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The facelifted GLC 300 e is a massive trap for uninformed buyers. While every other petrol facelift GLC received the newer, much more robust M264 engine, Mercedes strangely decided to keep the older, highly flawed M274 engine inside the GLC 300 e. This means that despite paying a premium for a newer facelift model, you still suffer from the catastrophic intake camshaft adjuster (VVT) rattle and timing chain stretch issues. Worse, this fragile engine is now violently slammed with 700 Nm of torque every time the hybrid system demands full power. Furthermore, you face massive repair bills for common high-voltage failures, such as the On-Board Charger (OBC) dying or the PTC cabin heater burning out. While the 13.5 kWh battery is better than the older model, the combination of the hidden, flawed M274 engine and intense hybrid complexity makes this vehicle a massive financial liability out of warranty. It earns a highly cautionary 8/10 risk rating.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "1,500 - 3,000 EUR / year",
