    "drivingExperience": "The pre-facelift GLC 63 AMG is an absolute monster. The M177 4.0L V8 erupts on startup with a deep, thunderous bellow that sets off car alarms. With 476 hp and 650 Nm of torque, acceleration is brutal and unrelenting. The 9-speed MCT transmission uses a wet start-up clutch instead of a torque converter, delivering lightning-fast, visceral gear changes that violently shove you into the seat. The 4MATIC+ system dynamically shuffles power to the rear, allowing for incredibly tail-happy, aggressive handling. It perfectly merges the utility of an SUV with the soul of a muscle car.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The GLC 63 AMG is mechanically incredible, but financially punishing to maintain. The core M177 V8 block is virtually indestructible, but its peripherals are a nightmare. The 'Hot-V' turbocharger layout bakes the engine valley, turning wiring and vacuum lines brittle. Your absolute priority is replacing the PCV oil separators proactively; if they fail, they overpressurize the crankcase and physically blow out the Rear Main Seal (RMS), resulting in a massive oil leak that requires removing the transmission to fix. You MUST install aftermarket mesh grilles to protect the front radiators from rock punctures. Finally, understand that the MCT wet clutch sheds friction material directly into the transmission fluid—you must service the transmission and the electronic locking rear diff strictly every 50,000 km. It is a masterpiece, but it earns a 7/10 risk rating due to the astronomical cost of AMG parts and labor.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "2,000 - 4,000 EUR / year",
