    "riskRating": 7,
    "drivingExperience": "The Facelift GLC 63 AMG combines brutal V8 performance with highly refined technology. The M177 4.0L V8 erupts on startup with a deep, thunderous bellow. The facelift introduced heavily updated software for the 9-speed MCT transmission and the electronic locking rear differential, making the car much smoother in low-speed city traffic while maintaining lightning-fast, visceral gear changes when pushed. With 476 hp and 650 Nm of torque dynamically sent to all four wheels, the acceleration is incredibly violent, perfectly merging the utility of an SUV with the soul of a muscle car.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The facelifted GLC 63 AMG is mechanically incredible, but financially punishing to maintain out of warranty. The core M177 V8 block is virtually indestructible, but its peripherals are highly stressed. The 'Hot-V' turbocharger layout bakes the engine valley, turning wiring and vacuum lines brittle. Your absolute priority is replacing the PCV oil separators proactively; if they fail, they overpressurize the crankcase and physically blow out the Rear Main Seal (RMS), resulting in a massive oil leak that requires removing the transmission to fix. You MUST install aftermarket mesh grilles to protect the front radiators from rock punctures. Finally, understand that the MCT wet clutch sheds friction material directly into the transmission fluid—you must service the transmission and the electronic locking rear diff strictly every 50,000 km. It earns a 7/10 risk rating due to the astronomical cost of AMG parts and specialized labor.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "2,000 - 4,000 EUR / year",
