    "riskRating": 7,
    "drivingExperience": "The GLC 63 S AMG is a brutally fast, apex predator in the SUV world. The 510 hp M177 4.0L V8 erupts on startup with an earth-shaking bellow. With 700 Nm of torque available almost instantly, acceleration is violent, pinning you deep into your seat. The 9-speed MCT transmission uses a wet start-up clutch instead of a torque converter, delivering lightning-fast, rifle-bolt gear changes. The S variant benefits from dynamic active engine mounts that stiffen the chassis under hard cornering, and the 4MATIC+ system dynamically shuffles power to the rear, allowing for incredibly tail-happy, aggressive handling. It perfectly merges the utility of a crossover with the soul of a muscle car.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The M177 4.0L V8 Biturbo in the GLC 63 S is an absolute engineering marvel, delivering supercar-level performance wrapped in a practical crossover body. As the fully unlocked 510 horsepower 'S' variant, it pushes the limits of modern internal combustion. The core of this engine—the closed-deck aluminum block and forged rotating assembly—is virtually indestructible and can easily handle over 700 horsepower. However, owning this masterpiece out of warranty requires a massive financial commitment, as its peripherals are notoriously fragile and incredibly expensive to replace. The 'Hot-V' turbocharger configuration creates a thermal nightmare under the hood, baking the wiring harnesses, ignition coils, and plastic vacuum lines until they become brittle and shatter. Your absolute highest priority as an owner is the PCV (Positive Crankcase Ventilation) system. The factory oil separators are prone to failure; when they clog, crankcase pressure rises exponentially and physically blows out the Rear Main Seal (RMS). Fixing a blown RMS requires dropping the entire transmission and exhaust system, resulting in a devastating labor bill. You must replace the PCV modules proactively every 60,000 km. Furthermore, the 'S' model features dynamic liquid-filled engine mounts which fail over time and are exceptionally expensive to replace. You must also install aftermarket mesh grilles immediately, as the massive front intakes leave the primary radiators completely exposed to catastrophic rock punctures. Finally, the AMG SPEEDSHIFT MCT transmission uses a wet multi-plate clutch instead of a torque converter; it sheds friction material directly into the oil, making strict 50,000 km fluid changes absolutely mandatory to prevent violent clutch shudder. If you maintain it obsessively, it is the ultimate SUV; if you neglect it, it will financially ruin you.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "2,000 - 4,500 EUR / year",
