    "author": ".",
    "riskRating": 7,
    "drivingExperience": "The Facelift GLC 63 S AMG combines brutal V8 performance with highly refined technology. The 510 hp M177 4.0L V8 erupts on startup with a deep, thunderous bellow. The facelift introduced the heavily updated AMG DYNAMICS software, optimizing the MCT transmission and the electronic locking rear differential to make the car much smoother in low-speed city traffic while maintaining lightning-fast, visceral gear changes when pushed. With 700 Nm of torque and dynamic engine mounts stiffening the chassis under load, the acceleration is incredibly violent. It perfectly merges the utility of an SUV with the handling and soul of a muscle car.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The M177 4.0L V8 Biturbo in the facelifted GLC 63 S is an absolute engineering marvel, delivering supercar-level performance wrapped in a practical crossover body. As the fully unlocked 510 horsepower 'S' variant, it pushes the limits of modern internal combustion. The core of this engine—the closed-deck aluminum block and forged rotating assembly—is virtually indestructible and can easily handle over 700 horsepower. However, owning this masterpiece out of warranty requires a massive financial commitment, as its peripherals are notoriously fragile and incredibly expensive to replace. The 'Hot-V' turbocharger configuration creates a thermal nightmare under the hood, baking the wiring harnesses, ignition coils, and plastic vacuum lines until they become brittle and shatter. Your absolute highest priority as an owner is the PCV (Positive Crankcase Ventilation) system. The factory oil separators are prone to failure; when they clog, crankcase pressure rises exponentially and physically blows out the Rear Main Seal (RMS). Fixing a blown RMS requires dropping the entire transmission and exhaust system, resulting in a devastating labor bill. You must replace the PCV modules proactively every 60,000 km. Furthermore, the 'S' model features dynamic liquid-filled engine mounts which fail over time and are exceptionally expensive to replace. You must also install aftermarket mesh grilles immediately, as the massive front intakes leave the primary radiators completely exposed to catastrophic rock punctures. Finally, the AMG SPEEDSHIFT MCT transmission uses a wet multi-plate clutch instead of a torque converter; it sheds friction material directly into the oil, making strict 50,000 km fluid changes absolutely mandatory to prevent violent clutch shudder. If you maintain it obsessively, it is the ultimate SUV; if you neglect it, it will financially ruin you.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "2,000 - 4,500 EUR / year",
    "model3DHotspots": [
      {
        "label": "Hot-V Twin Turbochargers",
        "x": 0,
        "y": 0.07,
        "z": 0.44,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Mounted directly inside the 'V' of the engine under the center of the hood. Creates immense heat that bakes the engine wiring harness and PCV lines."
      },
      {
        "label": "Rear Main Seal (RMS) / PCV Area",
        "x": 0,
        "y": 0.07,
        "z": 0.29,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Lower firewall area. If the PCV system fails, massive crankcase pressure blows this seal out, causing an oil leak that requires transmission removal."
      },
      {
        "label": "AMG Performance Exhaust Actuators",
        "x": -0.16,
        "y": -0.12,
        "z": -0.62,
        "critical": false,
        "details": "Rear exhaust tips. The electronic flap actuators seize or their internal springs break due to intense exhaust heat."
      },
      {
        "label": "Front Radiator & Intercooler Pack",
        "x": -0.25,
        "y": -0.11,
        "z": 0.41,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Behind the front bumper. The massive factory intakes lack mesh protection, making the radiators highly vulnerable to catastrophic rock punctures."
      },
      {
        "label": "Electronic Locking Rear Differential",
        "x": 0.26,
        "y": -0.11,
        "z": -0.38,
        "critical": false,
        "details": "Rear axle center. Highly stressed by the massive V8 torque. Requires fluid changes every 50,000 km to protect the clutch plates."
      },
      {
        "label": "AMG SPEEDSHIFT MCT 9G Transmission",
        "x": -0.26,
        "y": -0.11,
        "z": -0.37,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Underbody. Uses a wet multi-plate start-up clutch. Very sensitive to fluid degradation; strict 50k km fluid changes are mandatory."
      }
    ]
