    "author": ".",
    "riskRating": 6,
    "drivingExperience": "Driving the facelifted GLC 200 d reveals an incredibly smooth, highly refined diesel experience. The OM654 engine is vastly quieter and less agricultural than the older OM651. While 163 horsepower sounds remarkably low for a heavy SUV, the 360 Nm of low-end torque and the brilliant 9G-Tronic transmission mask the power deficit beautifully in city traffic. However, at highway speeds above 100 km/h, the artificial software restriction becomes glaringly obvious, and the car runs out of breath quickly during overtaking. The cabin is practically silent, making it a fantastic, ultra-efficient daily driver that simply begs for a Stage 1 tune to unlock its true potential.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The OM654 inside the facelifted GLC 200 d is a brilliant piece of core engineering hampered by devastating peripheral flaws. The good news: buying a 200 d and tuning it is the ultimate financial hack, as it is mechanically identical to the more expensive 220 d (194 hp); a simple software flash gives you 220 horsepower safely. The aluminum block and steel pistons are virtually indestructible, and the timing chain is vastly superior to the older OM651. The bad news: you must address its known flaws immediately. Your absolute first priority is inspecting the coolant shut-off valve. This cheap plastic valve leaks internally, pushing coolant through the vacuum lines directly into the brake booster, destroying your brakes and vacuum pump—a massive 2,000+ EUR repair. Preventatively replace it or install a vacuum check valve. Secondly, you must abandon the factory 0W-20 'eco' oil and 25,000 km intervals; use high-quality 5W-30 (MB 229.52) every 10,000 km to protect the fragile roller rocker arms from wearing down the camshafts. Finally, the AdBlue (SCR) system is a ticking financial time bomb—NOx sensors fail constantly, resulting in a no-start countdown. If you proactively address the coolant valve and use the right oil, the engine is phenomenal. Neglect it, and the repair bills will rival the cost of the car. It earns a 6/10 risk rating.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "1,000 - 2,000 EUR / year",
