    "author": ".",
    "riskRating": 6,
    "drivingExperience": "Driving the pre-facelift GLC 220 d offers a solid, if slightly uninspiring, diesel experience. The cast-iron OM651 engine is noticeably louder and more agricultural at idle compared to modern aluminum diesels. However, thanks to the sequential twin-turbocharger setup, turbo lag is virtually non-existent; you get a healthy 400 Nm shove right off the line. While 170 horsepower is objectively slow on the highway for an SUV of this weight, the brilliant 9G-Tronic transmission does an excellent job of keeping the engine in its optimal torque band. It is a highly efficient, torque-rich daily driver that simply begs for a Stage 1 software tune to unlock the 230 horsepower it is mechanically capable of delivering.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The OM651 inside the pre-facelift GLC 220 d is a legendary workhorse wrapped in a minefield of expensive flaws. From a performance standpoint, buying the 220 d and tuning it is a brilliant financial move—it is physically identical to the 204 hp 250 d, meaning a simple ECU flash safely unlocks 230 horsepower. The core cast-iron block is immortal and will easily outlast the chassis. However, you must be prepared to tackle its notorious vulnerabilities. The absolute biggest threat is the rear-mounted timing chain. Because it is buried against the firewall, replacing a stretched chain requires dropping the transmission or removing the engine entirely—a massive 1,500+ EUR labor bill. You MUST abandon the factory 25,000 km oil intervals and change your oil strictly every 10,000-15,000 km to prevent chain stretch. Secondly, you MUST physically unplug or software-delete the variable oil pump solenoid; if it sticks, your engine will lose oil pressure and spin a main bearing, destroying the block. Finally, the fragile AdBlue (SCR) system is a ticking time bomb—heater elements and NOx sensors fail constantly, triggering a countdown that will brick the car. If you proactively manage the oil pump solenoid, shorten your oil intervals, and address the AdBlue system (where legally permissible), this engine will run forever. Neglect it, and the labor bills will bury you. It earns a manageable 6/10 risk rating.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "1,000 - 2,200 EUR / year",
