    "timingType": "Simplex timing chain located at the REAR of the engine block against the firewall. This is the OM651's most critical and infamous design flaw. If the chain stretches due to extended 25,000 km oil intervals, replacing the chain and guides requires dropping the transmission or pulling the entire engine out of the chassis.",
    "injectionType": "Common Rail Direct Injection (CRDI). Utilizes highly reliable Delphi solenoid injectors. Far less problematic than the early piezo injectors used on the previous W204 generation OM651 engines.",
    "engineLifespan": "300,000 - 500,000+ km. The heavy cast-iron block and forged rotating assembly are legendary for their brute, commercial-grade strength (used extensively in Sprinter vans). However, reaching high mileage requires proactive timing chain replacement and permanently fixing the oil pump solenoid flaw.",
    "turboType": "Sequential Twin-Turbocharger (BorgWarner R2S). A small high-pressure turbo provides instant response off the line, while a larger low-pressure turbo takes over at higher RPMs. Produces 400 Nm of torque with virtually zero lag, even on this detuned fleet model.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch EDC17. A highly tunable and robust engine management system. On the 'Fleet Edition', the ECU runs an artificially restricted software map to limit output to 163 hp for European corporate tax bracket optimization.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "280+ hp. The internal rotating assembly is massively over-engineered. Because the 163 hp 'Fleet Edition' is mechanically identical to the 204 hp 250 d, the power deficit is entirely a software limitation, leaving immense headroom for aftermarket tuning.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "7.0 - 8.0 L/100km",
      "highway": "5.5 - 6.5 L/100km",
      "mixed": "6.0 - 7.0 L/100km",
      "sporty": "8.5+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "6.5L 5W-30 Fully Synthetic Low-SAPS (MB 229.51 or MB 229.52). Must use Low-SAPS to protect the DPF.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Rear Timing Chain Stretch: Extended oil intervals cause the single-row rear timing chain to stretch. A 2-3 second rattling sound on cold starts is the first warning sign.",
      "Variable Oil Pump Solenoid Failure: The eco-focused variable oil pump control solenoid can stick, causing dangerously low oil pressure under load.",
      "Water Pump & Coolant Flange Leaks: The plastic water pump housing and the coolant connection pipe located under the fuel filter frequently crack and leak coolant.",
      "Intake Manifold Swirl Flap Failure: Plastic linkages on the intake manifold wear out and break, causing boost leaks, poor fuel economy, and Limp Mode.",
      "AdBlue Tank Heater Failure: The internal heating element in the DEF (AdBlue) tank burns out, triggering a Check Engine Light and a no-start countdown.",
      "DPF Pressure Sensor Failure: Condensation ruins the differential pressure sensors, causing erratic regenerations."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Catastrophic Engine Seizure (Spun Bearings): If the variable oil pump solenoid fails in the 'low pressure' state during high engine load, the crankshaft main bearings will spin, destroying the iron engine block completely.",
      "Timing Chain Jump / Valvetrain Destruction: Ignoring the cold-start chain rattle will eventually cause the stretched chain to skip a tooth on the camshaft sprockets, resulting in piston-to-valve contact.",
      "Engine-Out Maintenance Bill: Because the timing chain is mounted flush against the firewall, replacing it requires dropping the transmission or pulling the engine, resulting in an astronomical 1,500+ EUR labor bill.",
      "SCR System Meltdown (Countdown): If any part of the AdBlue system fails, the ECU initiates a strict 'Starts Remaining' countdown. Once it reaches zero, the vehicle is electronically bricked.",
      "Turbocharger Bearing Failure: Chronic DPF blockage creates massive exhaust backpressure, which prevents the twin turbos from spooling freely and quickly destroys their bearings.",
      "Conductor Plate Failure (9G-Tronic): If the automatic transmission fluid is neglected, metallic debris will destroy the delicate speed sensors inside the transmission conductor plate."
    ],
