    "yearRange": "2019-2022",
    "timingType": "Simplex timing chain located at the REAR of the engine block. While vastly superior and much more durable than the infamous OM651 chain, its placement means that if replacement is ever required, the transmission must be dropped or the engine removed. Strict oil maintenance is mandatory.",
    "injectionType": "Common Rail Direct Injection (CRDI) via Bosch Piezo injectors operating at an incredibly high 2,050 Bar. Highly efficient, utilizing stepped-bowl combustion chambers for reduced soot.",
    "engineLifespan": "250,000 - 400,000+ km. The OM654 is a spectacular piece of engineering—Mercedes' first all-aluminum 4-cylinder diesel. The core block is practically immortal, but the lifespan of the vehicle is heavily threatened by the fragile SCR (AdBlue) system and a specific coolant-to-vacuum leak vulnerability.",
    "turboType": "Single Variable Nozzle Turbine (VNT) with water-cooled bearings. Extremely fast spooling, providing peak 400 Nm torque from just 1,600 RPM. Provides much better top-end breathing than the restricted 200 d model.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch MD1. A highly complex, multi-core engine management system that runs the fully unlocked factory tune for the single-turbo 2.0L diesel variant. Tightly integrates with the complex Euro 6d emissions hardware.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6d",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "240+ hp. The internal rotating assembly is incredibly robust (steel pistons in an aluminum block). The engine easily handles Stage 1 tuning without stressing the bottom end.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "6.8 - 7.8 L/100km",
      "highway": "5.2 - 6.2 L/100km",
      "mixed": "5.8 - 6.8 L/100km",
      "sporty": "9.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "6.5L 0W-20 or 5W-30 Fully Synthetic Ultra Low-SAPS (MB 229.71 or MB 229.52). 0W-20 is often used for emissions, but high-quality 5W-30 (MB 229.52) is strongly recommended by mechanics for better wear protection.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Coolant Valve Vacuum Leak: The most notorious OM654 flaw. The coolant shut-off valve leaks internally, pushing coolant directly into the vacuum system lines, destroying the vacuum pump and brake booster.",
      "Camshaft Rocker Arm Wear: Early OM654 engines can suffer from premature wear on the roller rocker arm bearings, scoring the camshaft lobes.",
      "NOx Sensor Failure: The dual NOx sensors fail frequently due to urea crystallization or soot, triggering an immediate Check Engine Light.",
      "AdBlue Injector Clogging: The AdBlue dosing nozzle in the exhaust stream crystalizes and clogs, preventing DEF injection and triggering the no-start countdown.",
      "EGR Valve Soot Buildup: The multi-way EGR system chokes with soot if the vehicle is exclusively driven on short city trips, leading to Limp Mode.",
      "Tandem Oil Pump Valve Sticking: The variable oil pump solenoid can stick, causing low oil pressure warnings.",
      "DPF Pressure Sensor Failure: Condensation ruins the DPF differential pressure sensor, preventing active regenerations."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Catastrophic Brake Booster Failure (Coolant Leak): If the coolant shut-off valve fails and pushes coolant through the vacuum lines, it will flood the vacuum pump and brake booster, leading to a sudden, terrifying loss of braking assistance and a 2,000+ EUR repair bill.",
      "AdBlue / SCR System Meltdown (Countdown): If any component of the complex Euro 6d AdBlue system fails, the ECU initiates a strict 'Starts Remaining' countdown. Once zero is reached, the car electronically bricks itself.",
      "Valvetrain Destruction (Rocker Arms): If the roller rocker arms fail and the noise is ignored, they will grind the camshaft lobes down to bare metal, sending steel shavings through the entire engine.",
      "Rear Timing Chain Labor Cost: Although rare, if the timing chain stretches due to extended 25,000 km oil intervals, the engine or transmission must be removed—resulting in a massive labor bill.",
      "Conductor Plate Failure (9G-Tronic): Neglecting the 80,000 km transmission fluid service will allow metallic debris to short out the sensitive internal solenoids on the 9G-Tronic conductor plate.",
      "DPF Thermal Cracking: Repeated, failed DPF regenerations caused by city driving or a bad pressure sensor will cause the DPF core to melt or crack, requiring a hugely expensive replacement."
    ],
