    "author": ".",
    "riskRating": 6,
    "drivingExperience": "Driving the pre-facelift GLC 250 d delivers the quintessential Mercedes diesel experience. The sequential twin-turbocharged OM651 engine offers a massive wave of 500 Nm torque starting from incredibly low RPMs, completely eliminating turbo lag. This allows the heavy X253 chassis to feel surprisingly light and punchy in city traffic. While the cast-iron 4-cylinder is noticeably louder and rougher at idle compared to modern aluminum V6s, it quiets down beautifully at highway speeds. The 9G-Tronic transmission shifts early to ride the massive torque curve, making it a highly efficient, capable, and confident long-distance cruiser.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The OM651 inside the GLC 250 d is a legendary workhorse wrapped in a minefield of expensive engineering flaws. On the positive side, the 204 horsepower variant uses the maximum potential of the sequential twin-turbo setup, offering fantastic torque, and the core cast-iron block is immortal. However, you must be prepared to tackle its notorious vulnerabilities head-on. The absolute biggest threat is the rear-mounted timing chain. Because it is buried against the firewall, replacing a stretched chain requires dropping the transmission or removing the engine entirely—a massive 1,500+ EUR labor bill. You MUST abandon the factory 25,000 km oil intervals and change your oil strictly every 10,000-15,000 km to prevent chain stretch. \n\nSecondly, you MUST physically unplug or software-delete the variable oil pump solenoid; if this eco-feature sticks in the low-pressure state, your engine will spin a main bearing and destroy the block. Finally, the fragile AdBlue (SCR) system is a ticking time bomb—heater elements and NOx sensors fail constantly, triggering a countdown that will electronically brick the car. If you proactively manage the oil pump solenoid, shorten your oil intervals, and address the AdBlue system (where legally permissible), this engine will run for half a million kilometers. Neglect it, and the labor bills will bury you. It earns a manageable 6/10 risk rating.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "1,000 - 2,200 EUR / year",
