    "author": ".",
    "riskRating": 6,
    "drivingExperience": "Driving the facelifted GLC 300 d reveals an incredibly smooth, violently fast, and highly refined diesel experience. The Biturbo OM654 engine is vastly superior to the older OM651. With 245 horsepower and 500 Nm of torque, it pulls the heavy SUV effortlessly. The dual-turbo setup completely eliminates low-end lag while providing phenomenal top-end breathing, allowing it to accelerate hard well beyond 160 km/h. The brilliant 9G-Tronic transmission executes shifts seamlessly. The cabin is practically silent, making it a fantastic, ultra-fast daily driver that perfectly balances Mercedes luxury with tremendous diesel fuel economy.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The Biturbo OM654 inside the facelifted GLC 300 d is a brilliant piece of core engineering hampered by devastating peripheral flaws. The aluminum block and steel pistons are virtually indestructible, and the timing chain architecture is vastly superior to the older OM651. However, because this is the maximum-output 300 d version, the fuel system is operating at a mind-bending 2,500 Bar; poor quality fuel will destroy the HPFP, sending shrapnel through the entire system and causing a catastrophic repair bill. \n\nBeyond fueling, you must address its known mechanical flaws immediately. Your absolute first priority is inspecting the coolant shut-off valve. This cheap plastic valve leaks internally, pushing coolant through the vacuum lines directly into the brake booster, destroying your brakes and vacuum pump—a massive 2,000+ EUR repair. Preventatively replace it or install a vacuum check valve. Secondly, you must abandon the factory 0W-20 'eco' oil and 25,000 km intervals; use high-quality 5W-30 (MB 229.52) every 10,000 km to protect the fragile roller rocker arms from the massive thermal load of the twin turbos. Finally, the AdBlue (SCR) system is a ticking financial time bomb—NOx sensors fail constantly, resulting in a no-start countdown. If you proactively address the coolant valve and use the right oil, the engine is phenomenal. Neglect it, and it will bankrupt you. It earns a 6/10 risk rating.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "1,000 - 2,500 EUR / year",
