    "author": ".",
    "riskRating": 8,
    "drivingExperience": "Driving the GLC 300 de is an experience of two vastly different personalities. With a charged battery, it is an incredibly smooth, silent EV that glides through city traffic on electric power alone. The instant 440 Nm of electric torque makes it feel light and agile off the line. However, when you floor the throttle and the 2.0L diesel engine violently awakens, the combined 700 Nm of torque shoves you into the seat, delivering acceleration that rivals high-performance V6s. The transition between electric and diesel power is generally smooth but can be slightly jarring under sudden, heavy loads. The massive downside is weight—at over 2,100 kg, the car feels heavy in corners, and when the battery is completely depleted, the 4-cylinder diesel has to drag around hundreds of kilos of dead hybrid hardware.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The GLC 300 de is an absolute technological marvel that creates a terrifying nightmare for long-term ownership out of warranty. Mercedes took one of the most mechanically complex architectures available—a modern Euro 6d clean diesel with DPF, SCR, AdBlue, and precise valvetrain requirements—and mated it to a highly complex, heavy high-voltage EV system. \n\nMechanically, you face the standard OM654 ticking time bombs: the plastic coolant shut-off valve that WILL eventually leak and destroy your brake booster (a 2,000 EUR fix), and the fragile AdBlue NOx sensors that trigger no-start countdowns. But the hybrid integration makes things much worse. Because the car relies on the electric motor in the city, the diesel engine rarely reaches operating temperature. This cold, incomplete combustion creates massive amounts of soot that clogs the DPF and EGR systems incredibly fast. Furthermore, when the battery suddenly dies on the highway, the cold diesel engine is instantly forced to full RPM to take over, causing brutal wear on the camshaft roller rocker arms.\n\nElectrically, the On-Board Charger (OBC) is prone to failure, and the 13.5 kWh battery degrades, severely impacting range and leaving you with a very heavy, inefficient diesel SUV. The immense weight also rapidly destroys the air suspension and front control arms. If you buy this car, you must take it on a fast 30-minute highway run every week to burn off the diesel soot, and you must proactively replace the coolant valve. It earns a highly cautionary 8/10 risk rating.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "2,000 - 4,000 EUR / year",
