    "author": ".",
    "riskRating": 7,
    "drivingExperience": "The pre-facelift GLC 350 d delivers an incredibly smooth, locomotive-like driving experience. The massive 3.0L V6 is dramatically quieter and more refined at idle than the 4-cylinder diesels. When you step on the gas, the 620 Nm of torque surges forward effortlessly, easily masking the heavy weight of the SUV. It lacks the highly strung, rev-happy nature of a petrol engine, instead relying on immense low-end grunt that makes highway overtaking an absolute breeze. The 9G-Tronic transmission pairs perfectly with this engine, keeping it exactly in its massive torque band. It is the ultimate long-distance towing and highway cruising machine.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The OM642 3.0L V6 diesel is a legend in the Mercedes lineup, known equally for its bulletproof cast-iron block and its financially devastating peripheral oil leaks. Mechanically, the engine bottom-end is immortal. However, owning this out of warranty requires knowing exactly what you are dealing with. \n\nThe absolute most critical flaw is the oil cooler seals. The cooler sits deep inside the 'V' of the engine. The factory seals degrade, causing oil to pour down the back of the engine. The seals cost 20 EUR, but replacing them requires removing the turbocharger, intake manifolds, and EGR system—a brutal 1,500+ EUR labor job. Secondly, the PCV breather valve fails, pulling oil vapor into the turbo inlet. This oil drips down and drowns the M55 swirl flap motor, shorting it out and throwing the car into Limp Mode. You MUST replace the PCV valve and the red turbo seal proactively every 60,000 km. \n\nFinally, the AdBlue (SCR) system is a ticking time bomb—tank heaters and NOx sensors fail frequently, triggering a no-start countdown that will electronically brick the car. If you can stomach the cost of the oil cooler seal job and proactively maintain the PCV system, the engine will easily surpass 400,000 km. If you neglect it, the oil leaks and electronic faults will bleed your wallet dry. It earns a cautionary 7/10 risk rating.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "1,500 - 3,000 EUR / year",
    "model3DHotspots": [
      {
        "label": "V-Valley Oil Cooler",
        "x": 0.0,
        "y": 0.15,
        "z": 0.30,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Deep inside the center 'V' of the engine block beneath the turbo. The seals fail and cause massive oil leaks. A 20 EUR part that requires a 1,500 EUR labor teardown to replace."
      },
      {
        "label": "Turbocharger & Red Inlet Seal",
        "x": 0.0,
        "y": 0.20,
        "z": 0.35,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Top center of the engine. The red silicone seal frequently leaks oil. If this oil drips down, it destroys the swirl flap motor."
      },
      {
        "label": "Swirl Flap Motor (M55)",
        "x": 0.0,
        "y": 0.10,
        "z": 0.35,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Located beneath the turbo inlet. If oil drips onto it from a bad PCV seal, it shorts out the CAN-bus and forces the car into Limp Mode."
      },
      {
        "label": "AdBlue / SCR System & NOx Sensors",
        "x": -0.16,
        "y": -0.12,
        "z": -0.62,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Exhaust system. The fragile AdBlue tank heater and NOx sensors fail frequently. If they fail, a 'Starts Remaining' no-start countdown begins."
      },
      {
        "label": "Front Timing Cover / Chain",
        "x": 0.0,
        "y": 0.10,
        "z": 0.50,
        "critical": false,
        "details": "Front of the engine block. The timing chain can stretch on high-mileage examples (200,000+ km), causing a noticeable cold-start rattle."
      },
      {
        "label": "PCV Crankcase Breather Valve",
        "x": 0.15,
        "y": 0.20,
        "z": 0.40,
        "critical": false,
        "details": "Top right cylinder head cover. Fails frequently, causing excessive oil vapor to be ingested by the turbocharger. Replace every 60k km."
      }
    ]
