    "author": ".",
    "riskRating": 6,
    "drivingExperience": "The GLC 400 d facelift is nothing short of a diesel masterpiece. Moving from the old OM642 V6 to the new OM656 Inline-6 eliminated virtually all diesel clatter, resulting in an exceptionally smooth, highly refined idle. But the real magic happens when you press the accelerator. With 330 horsepower and a devastating 700 Nm of torque available almost instantly, the heavy SUV launches forward with terrifying force. The biturbo setup completely eliminates turbo lag, and it pulls relentlessly well past 200 km/h. It feels like a genuine AMG product, yet it can easily return 7.5 L/100km on the highway. It is the ultimate balance of raw performance and long-distance efficiency.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The OM656 2.9L Inline-6 inside the GLC 400 d is a monumental upgrade over the old, leak-prone OM642 V6. Gone are the infamous V-valley oil cooler leaks and the plastic swirl flap linkages. The core of this Inline-6—aluminum block with Nanoslide coating and steel pistons—is virtually immortal and easily handles the massive 330 horsepower output. \n\nHowever, it is not without flaws. The OM656 shares the fatal design flaw of its 4-cylinder sibling, the OM654: the plastic coolant shut-off valve. This valve leaks internally, pushing coolant directly into your vacuum system and destroying the brake booster—a massive, dangerous 2,000 EUR repair. You MUST replace it proactively or bypass it. Furthermore, you must abandon the 0W-20 factory 'eco' oil. Switch to a high-quality 5W-30 (MB 229.52) and change it every 10,000 km to protect the camshaft roller rocker arms from the extreme thermal load of the 400d's twin turbos. Finally, the AdBlue (SCR) system remains a fragile nuisance, prone to NOx sensor failures and countdown bricks. Address the coolant valve, use thick oil, and this engine is arguably the best diesel Mercedes has ever built. It earns a manageable 6/10 risk rating.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "1,000 - 2,500 EUR / year",
    "model3DHotspots": [
      {
        "label": "Coolant Shut-Off Valve (Vacuum Intrusion Risk)",
        "x": 0.0,
        "y": 0.10,
        "z": 0.50,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "CRITICAL FLAW: Front/side of the engine block. This plastic valve leaks internally, pushing coolant directly into the vacuum system lines. If it reaches the brake booster, you lose braking assist and face a massive repair bill."
      },
      {
        "label": "Rear Timing Chain Location",
        "x": 0.0,
        "y": 0.07,
        "z": 0.25,
        "critical": false,
        "details": "Firewall area. The timing chain is mounted at the rear of the engine block. While very durable on the I6, if it stretches, engine or transmission removal is required."
      },
      {
        "label": "Sequential Twin-Turbo Assembly",
        "x": -0.22,
        "y": 0.12,
        "z": 0.35,
        "critical": false,
        "details": "Passenger side of the engine (LHD). The OM656 uses a complex biturbo setup to generate the massive 700 Nm of torque. Very reliable, but heavily constrained by the DPF located immediately downstream."
      },
      {
        "label": "AdBlue / SCR System & NOx Sensors",
        "x": -0.16,
        "y": -0.12,
        "z": -0.62,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Exhaust system. The dual NOx sensors and AdBlue injector clog and fail frequently, triggering a 'Starts Remaining' countdown that will electronically brick the car."
      },
      {
        "label": "Camtronic Actuators & Rocker Arms",
        "x": 0.0,
        "y": 0.18,
        "z": 0.35,
        "critical": true,
        "details": "Under the valve cover. Controls variable exhaust valve lift. Using thin 0W-20 oil under the heavy 330 hp thermal load can cause the roller rocker arm bearings to fail."
      },
      {
        "label": "High-Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP)",
        "x": 0.15,
        "y": 0.10,
        "z": 0.30,
        "critical": false,
        "details": "Side of the engine. Operates at a massive 2,500 Bar. Highly sensitive to contaminated diesel fuel; change the fuel filter strictly every 40,000 km to prevent catastrophic shrapnel damage."
      }
    ]
