    "author": ".",
    "riskRating": 8,
    "drivingExperience": "Driving the GLC 300 e is an experience of two vastly different personalities. With a charged battery, it is an incredibly smooth, silent EV. Because Mercedes equipped the X254 with a massive 31.2 kWh battery (larger than many early full EVs), it boasts an electric range of over 100 km, allowing most users to commute purely on electricity. The instant 440 Nm of electric torque makes it feel agile off the line. However, when you floor the throttle and the 2.0L petrol engine violently awakens, the combined 550 Nm of torque shoves you into the seat. The transition between electric and petrol power is generally smooth but can be slightly jarring under sudden, heavy loads. The massive downside is weight—at nearly 2,400 kg, the car feels incredibly heavy in corners. When the battery is completely depleted, the 4-cylinder engine has to drag around hundreds of kilos of dead hybrid hardware.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The GLC 300 e is an absolute technological marvel that creates a terrifying nightmare for long-term ownership out of warranty. Mercedes took a complex, high-output turbocharged 4-cylinder petrol engine and mated it to an EV system with a gargantuan 31.2 kWh battery pack. \n\nMechanically, you face the standard M254 ticking time bombs: complex electronic water pumps, rear-mounted timing chains, and PCV valve failures. But the hybrid integration makes things much worse. Because the car relies on the electric motor in the city, the petrol engine rarely reaches operating temperature. This cold, incomplete combustion creates carbon buildup on the intake valves and clogs the GPF (Gasoline Particulate Filter). Furthermore, when the battery suddenly dies on the highway, the cold petrol engine is instantly forced to full RPM to take over, causing brutal wear on the cylinder honing and valvetrain.\n\nElectrically, the On-Board Charger (OBC) is prone to failure, and the massive battery degrades, severely impacting range and leaving you with a very heavy, inefficient SUV. The immense weight also rapidly destroys the air suspension and front control arms, and overloads the standard braking system. If you buy this car, you MUST take it on a fast 30-minute highway run every week to burn off the GPF soot, and you MUST change the oil every 10,000 km with 5W-30 to protect the cold-started engine. It earns a highly cautionary 8/10 risk rating.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "2,000 - 4,500 EUR / year",
