    "author": ".",
    "riskRating": 8,
    "drivingExperience": "The GLC 400 e is the ultimate sleeper. Visually, it looks like a standard luxury SUV, but with 381 horsepower and 650 Nm of combined torque, it accelerates with terrifying, relentless force. The 100 kW electric motor provides instant, silent thrust off the line, perfectly bridging the gap until the fully unlocked 252 hp petrol engine awakens with a fierce roar. The combination makes it faster than many older AMG models. Like the 300 e, the massive 31.2 kWh battery provides over 100 km of pure electric range, making it incredibly versatile. However, the immense 2,400 kg kerb weight cannot be ignored; while it is violently fast in a straight line, it feels heavy and unwieldy under hard cornering or heavy braking.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The GLC 400 e pushes the absolute physical limits of the X254 PHEV architecture, making it both awe-inspiring and terrifying out of warranty. Mercedes took the M254 engine, opened the software fully to 252 hp, and mated it to a gargantuan 31.2 kWh battery and electric motor. \n\nThis creates a perfect storm of thermal and mechanical stress. The ICE suffers from brutal high-RPM cold starts when the battery depletes on the highway. Because it runs the high-output map, the roller rocker arms and cylinders are subjected to immense heat and friction instantly. If you use the factory 0W-20 oil, you are begging for premature engine wear—you MUST switch to 5W-30 and change it every 10,000 km. \n\nBeyond the ICE, you face all the PHEV nightmares: the On-Board Charger (OBC) is prone to failure, and the massive battery degrades, costing 15,000+ EUR to replace. The immense 2.4-ton weight rapidly destroys the air suspension, control arms, and severely overloads the standard braking system. Finally, the infamous OM654/M254 coolant valve flaw remains—if it leaks into the vacuum lines, you lose your brakes. If you buy this car, maintain it obsessively, drive it on the highway weekly to clear the GPF, and pray the electrical systems hold up. It earns a highly cautionary 8/10 risk rating.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "2,500 - 5,000 EUR / year",
