    "author": ".",
    "riskRating": 9,
    "drivingExperience": "The GLC 63 S E Performance is a physics-defying technological monster. It completely abandons the iconic V8 for the most advanced 4-cylinder hybrid system on earth. The acceleration is violent and instantaneous; the moment you press the throttle, the 204 hp rear electric motor shoves you into the seat while the electric exhaust turbo simultaneously spools the 476 hp petrol engine to 2.6 bar of boost in milliseconds. The combined 1020 Nm of torque hits like a sledgehammer, making the massive SUV accelerate like a supercar. The rear-axle steering and AMG active roll stabilization desperately try to hide the crushing 2.3-ton kerb weight. It lacks the acoustic soul of the old V8, sounding more like an angry, synthesized spaceship, but its sheer speed and technological grip are absolutely devastating.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The GLC 63 S E Performance represents the absolute pinnacle of Mercedes engineering, and as a result, it is a ticking financial time bomb for anyone brave enough to own it out of warranty. This is not just a car; it is a networked collection of extremely stressed Formula 1 technology. \n\nMechanically, you have a 2.0L engine producing a staggering 476 horsepower (238 hp/liter). This requires flawless oil maintenance (5W-40 every 10k km) and religious warm-up procedures (90°C oil temp) before heavy throttle, otherwise the forged internals will tear themselves apart. Electrically, it is a nightmare: the rear axle houses an Electric Drive Unit (EDU) with its own 2-speed transmission and e-LSD. The 6.1 kWh High-Performance battery uses 14 liters of non-conductive fluid flowing directly over the cells. If a seal leaks, the battery bricks itself. The electric turbocharger is brilliant, but an actuator failure means a 4,000 EUR bill. \n\nEvery single component on this car is operating at the absolute limit of modern material science. A failure in the MCT wet clutch, the rear EDU, or the hybrid network will result in repair bills that rival the cost of a small car. It is a masterpiece of speed, but it earns an extreme 9/10 risk rating for long-term ownership.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "4,000 - 8,000 EUR / year",
