    "riskRating": 5,
    "drivingExperience": "The GLC 450 d with the OM656M Inline-6 engine is arguably the finest long-distance cruiser Mercedes makes. The inherent balance of the Inline-6 layout means zero primary or secondary engine vibrations—it is velvety smooth. With 367 horsepower and 750 Nm of torque backed by the 48V ISG electric motor, acceleration is effortless, silent, and immensely powerful. The engine emits a refined, muted turbine hum rather than a harsh diesel clatter. Combined with the 9G-Tronic transmission, it eats up hundreds of kilometers on the autobahn with supreme grace and exceptional fuel economy.",
    "mechanicVerdict": "The OM656M inside the GLC 450 d is a masterpiece of modern diesel engineering. Unlike the stressed 4-cylinder diesels, this Inline-6 layout is inherently balanced and vastly over-engineered. The core aluminum block with steel pistons is practically bulletproof, easily capable of 400,000+ km if maintained correctly. \n\nHowever, you must still contend with modern diesel and hybrid complexities. First, the 48V ISG is integrated into the transmission bellhousing; if it fails, dropping the transmission is a 2,500+ EUR labor job. Second, the timing chain is mounted at the rear against the firewall—protect it by using 5W-30 oil and changing it every 15,000 km, ignoring factory 25k intervals. Third, the Bosch CP4 high-pressure fuel pump requires clean, lubricated diesel; change your fuel filter every 30k km religiously. Finally, the dual SCR (AdBlue) system requires occasional attention. Overall, it is the most reliable and refined engine choice in the X254 lineup, earning a very respectable 5/10 risk rating.",
    "repairCostEstimate": "1,200 - 2,500 EUR / year",
